I was on the math team when I was in junior high

511
00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:04.130
and academic teams and I credit my dad for being the one

512
00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:06.450
that, I mean, I used to help my dad

513
00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.970
with baseball cards and learning baseball stats.

514
00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:11.670
And so, when I got into running, I'm learning running

515
00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:15.420
stats and, you know, times, and track and field.

516
00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.600
And, yeah, I'm a total, like, running dork, so.

517
00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.369
Right, right, right.

518
00:22:20.369 --> 00:22:23.790
So, you go on to this illustrious career on the road.

519
00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:26.790
I mean, you've won like 21 marathons, is that right?

520
00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:27.623
Yeah.

521
00:22:27.623 --> 00:22:30.540
And you won Comrades, that was in 2017?

522
00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:31.373
Yeah.

523
00:22:31.373 --> 00:22:32.790
I mean, that had to be a career highlight,

524
00:22:32.790 --> 00:22:33.623
winning that crazy race.

525
00:22:33.623 --> 00:22:34.743
Oh yeah, oh yeah.

526
00:22:35.653 --> 00:22:38.207
I actually, after I won Comrades, I thought I could retire.

527
00:22:38.207 --> 00:22:39.420
(Camille laughing)

528
00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:40.353
Yeah, I mean, what's left?

529
00:22:40.353 --> 00:22:42.840
With all those thousands of people cheering you on.

530
00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:45.630
I mean, I can't imagine much could live up to that.

531
00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:49.950
No, I, yeah, I literally, the month after that race,

532
00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:52.960
I was like, I think I can retire now.

533
00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:57.660
And I had to reformulate my goals and I realized like,

534
00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:00.780
oh, there's all these world records I can go after.

535
00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:03.690
And, you know, that was kind of at the beginning

536
00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:05.970
of my ultra career and a lot of people

537
00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.130
were trying to push me into trail running

538
00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:11.220
and I started getting my feet wet and doing trail running,

539
00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:13.200
but I really had to follow my heart

540
00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:16.380
and my heart was telling me to go after the world records.

541
00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.170
So, after I won Comrades in June,

542
00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:21.570
later in the fall is when I started studying,

543
00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:24.420
like, the 12 hour, 100 mile world records.

544
00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:26.610
And, yeah, that was, you know,

545
00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:28.549
what started me like, yeah.

546
00:23:28.549 --> 00:23:30.647
You're like, this is easy.

547
00:23:30.647 --> 00:23:31.480
(Camille laughing)

548
00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:33.180
This is like low hanging fruit for me.

549
00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:36.780
I think it's unique and interesting that you're coming

550
00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:40.980
into ultra running with this illustrious career on the road.

551
00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:42.570
So, you have all this speed, right?

552
00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:45.249
Like a lot of people who get into ultra running

553
00:23:45.249 --> 00:23:47.520
don't have that type of background

554
00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:50.550
or that type of success in other areas of running.

555
00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:53.520
So, you're bringing this new elite level

556
00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.940
of, like, being able to just crush it

557
00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.580
in terms of pace that's new.

558
00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:02.670
And then, the question becomes, can that be extended, right?

559
00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.980
And you're proving, like, actually it can.

560
00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:06.930
And that's the way that you train also.

561
00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:09.930
You're still training like an elite marathoner,

562
00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:13.590
not like how we think about ultra distance trail runners

563
00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.690
where it's just go out and run really long

564
00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:18.150
as many times a week as you possibly can.

565
00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:19.830
Yeah, exactly.

566
00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:22.520
I think that that's what differentiates me

567
00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:25.317
is that I had a marathoning career for 10 years

568
00:24:25.317 --> 00:24:27.540
and I just brought my marathon training

569
00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:29.430
approach into ultra running.

570
00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:31.530
And, literally, for my first 100K,

571
00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.997
I broke my first record by Anne Trason.

572
00:24:33.997 --> 00:24:36.930
And, I mean, I was pretty naive.

573
00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:39.060
I didn't know how ultra runners train.

574
00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.910
I didn't even know who Anne Trason was.

575
00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:43.230
Like, I'd read about her.

576
00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.120
I guess everybody read "Born to Run"

577
00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:47.610
and I had read that back when I was a marathoner,

578
00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:49.410
but I didn't really know, like,

579
00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:52.680
anything about the sport, how the athletes train.

580
00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:55.200
I just came into the sport with a marathon

581
00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:58.200
training approach and started breaking records.

582
00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:01.020
And I figured, why change what works?

583
00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:03.380
And so, I've been able to go longer

584
00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:07.230
with marathon training and continue to break the records.

585
00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:09.060
And I haven't really found, you know,

586
00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:11.582
a limitation to the way that I train.

587
00:25:11.582 --> 00:25:13.830
Obviously, if I'm training for trail,

588
00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:16.020
I do more trail running, but I'm still, you know,

589
00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:19.110
that foundation of being a marathon training, you know,

590
00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:22.800
works for, it's worked all the way up to 24 hours so far.

591
00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:25.650
Right, and you're not doing crazy long runs.

592
00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:28.437
Like, your longest runs are between 18 and 22.

593
00:25:28.437 --> 00:25:30.930
And you only do that every couple weeks, right?

594
00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:33.960
Yeah, yeah, in fact, I was just telling my friend,

595
00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:36.900
Shannon, this morning, I was like, the longest training

596
00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:40.110
run I've done the past five years is 22 miles.

597
00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:41.670
Wow.

598
00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:43.620
Yeah, and so, I told her, I was like,

599
00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:46.710
the only time I ever go further than that is if I race.

600
00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:47.820
So, it's almost like the races.

601
00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:48.780
You are racing a lot.

602
00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:49.970
Yeah.

603
00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:50.803
So, that is part of the training.

604
00:25:50.803 --> 00:25:51.870
Yeah, definitely.

605
00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:54.820
I mean, you know, I just ran a 40 miler last weekend,

606
00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.020
you know, why do I need to do more long runs,

607
00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:59.970
you know, when I just ran 40 miles?

608
00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:03.606
So, I think, you know, racing is training.

609
00:26:03.606 --> 00:26:06.150
You know, racing develops your fitness as well.

610
00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:09.240
And so, I just really focus on recovering

611
00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:12.120
between my races and making sure I feel really good

612
00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:16.316
and do a lot of quality work, which is really important.

613
00:26:16.316 --> 00:26:17.850
Trying to keep in touch

614
00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:20.040
with my leg speed is super important.

615
00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.020
I find if I get away from my leg speed

616
00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.570
too much that I get a little bit flat.

617
00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:28.170
And so, you know, coming from a short distance background,

618
00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:30.242
I still kind of have to tap into that.

619
00:26:30.242 --> 00:26:32.430
Yeah, a couple observations with that,

620
00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:33.840
just so people aren't confused.

621
00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:35.190
It's not like you're not training.

622
00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.392
I mean, you run twice a day, most days, right?

623
00:26:37.392 --> 00:26:38.286
Yeah.

624
00:26:38.286 --> 00:26:41.160
So, you're still crossing over the a hundred miles

625
00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:43.500
a week mark pretty consistently, right?

626
00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:44.340
Yeah, yeah.

627
00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:47.820
I get in, you know, 100, 130 miles per week,

628
00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:51.850
but I do it in 12 to 13 runs a week.

629
00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:52.683
Right.

630
00:26:52.683 --> 00:26:55.950
So, yeah, I'm just doing mainly short frequent runs.

631
00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.810
And then, you know, every couple weeks

632
00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:00.243
I might do a long run or I'll do a race.

633
00:27:00.243 --> 00:27:02.280
But, really, it's just, you know,

634
00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:05.850
I feel like I recover really well with my training approach

635
00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.010
and I feel really good and energized

636
00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:10.230
and it makes me feel really fresh

637
00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.570
and strong when I go into races that

638
00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:15.619
I'm not beat down from a lot of long runs.

639
00:27:15.619 --> 00:27:19.440
And, yeah. in fact, when I first got into the sport,

640
00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:22.170
I mean, I kind of experimented with doing,

641
00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:26.460
you know, 30 mile plus training runs.

642
00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:29.340
And I found that I just felt tired trying to do that.

643
00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:32.040
So, when I went back down to shorten my long runs

644
00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:35.790
and went back down to only 18 to 22 mile long runs

645
00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:39.120
every couple weeks that's when I started to feel fresher.

646
00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:42.390
Right, fresher, able to bounce back more quickly.

647
00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.000
And that way your training sessions

648
00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.790
are of a higher quality, in general, right?

649
00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:48.623
Yeah.

650
00:27:48.623 --> 00:27:50.550
Your husband, Conor, is here.

651
00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:52.860
He's your coach, he has been for a long time.

652
00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.960
I'm interested in getting, like,

653
00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:57.060
really nitty gritty and detailed about the training.

654
00:27:57.060 --> 00:28:01.980
Like, how much of it is, what is the ratio of,

655
00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:05.490
like, the easy runs to the hard tempo speed work?

656
00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:08.340
Like, it appears that you train on the polarities.

657
00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.130
Either you're going really chill or you're,

658
00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:13.620
you know, crushing intervals at, you know,

659
00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:15.780
at the other end of that spectrum.

660
00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:18.339
Yeah, actually Strava is really helpful

661
00:28:18.339 --> 00:28:21.420
for knowing kind of the stats on my training.

662
00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:24.500
I think that, last year, it said that 86%

663
00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:28.830
of my training was easy and considered an easy pace.

664
00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:30.690
And what's easy?

665
00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:32.940
So, for me, like, I guess on road, that would

666
00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:35.910
be like an eight to nine minute per mile pace.

667
00:28:35.910 --> 00:28:38.700
On trail, it might be quite a bit slower.

668
00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:42.540
But, yeah, I mean, I'm going about 65 to 70%

669
00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:46.680
of max heart rate effort as my easy pace.

670
00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.240
And so, yeah, I mean, I'm doing, you know,

671
00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:52.650
86% is pretty aerobic, easy type of training.

672
00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:54.810
And then my hard days are very hard

673
00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:57.510
and, like, I might be doing, like,

674
00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:00.750
I don't know, anywhere between six to 18 miles

675
00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:03.870
at, like, a pretty hard, hard effort.

676
00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:07.020
And so, we operate pretty much on a two week

677
00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:10.140
training cycle where we have short intervals,

678
00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:13.980
long intervals, a hill session, and a progression run.

679
00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:18.390
So, in between those four main workouts, it's just easy,

680
00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:21.720
you know, aerobic training, running twice a day.

681
00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:25.620
And so, yeah, being a 24 hour runner,

682
00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:27.690
I have to be used to training, you know,

683
00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:30.150
multiple times a day and being comfortable, you know,

684
00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:31.800
early morning or late at night.

685
00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.920
And so, yeah, I just get myself comfortable for that.

686
00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:35.820
Right.

687
00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:38.730
So, yeah, you like to do the evening runs don't you?

688
00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:39.740
Yes.

689
00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:43.456
How much of the tempo and hard efforts are,

690
00:29:43.456 --> 00:29:46.860
like, track based or interval based, you know,

691
00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:49.080
in that kind of traditional track and field approach

692
00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:51.240
versus like, oh, I'm just going out on the road

693
00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:55.030
and it's gonna be six miles at this level of intensity?

694
00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:59.024
Yeah, I mean, I guess if I'm doing 14% of my training,

695
00:29:59.024 --> 00:30:02.310
I mean, I do like 90 second repeats

696
00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:05.550
is like kind of my short sprint type workouts.

697
00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:08.400
I mean, that's like, I usually do it on road, too.

698
00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:10.490
I don't do it on track, I hate the track.

699
00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:11.910
(Camille laughing)

700
00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:13.110
Which is really funny.

701
00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:14.700
I absolutely hate the track.

702
00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.040
I don't train on the track at all.

703
00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:21.644
So, I do everything on road and I do like 90 second repeats,

704
00:30:21.644 --> 00:30:25.950
maybe like five to six minute long intervals,

705
00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:29.220
sometimes, maybe two miles or three mile intervals,

706
00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:32.580
which might be like half marathon or marathon pace.

707
00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:35.100
And then, my progression runs might be anywhere

708
00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:38.400
between 30 to 60 minutes where I progress

709
00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:43.290
it from about 80% of max heart rate effort up to 90%.

710
00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:47.730
And so, yeah, when I race 50 miles to a 100K,

711
00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:52.290
I equate that to about 80% of max heart rate effort.

712
00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:54.150
And then, when I run a hundred miles,

713
00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:57.690
it's like 75% of max heart rate.

714
00:30:57.690 --> 00:30:59.460
And then, when we do 24 hours,

715
00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:01.380
that's more like a super chill,

716
00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:04.708
like 65 to 70% of max heart rate.

717
00:31:04.708 --> 00:31:08.040
And you wear a heart rate strap, chest strap,

718
00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:09.670
to gauge all of that and monitor it?

719
00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:11.520
Yeah, yeah.

720
00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:13.740
What's your max heart rate?

721
00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:16.110
Yeah, actually, I've kind of gotten,

722
00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:19.770
it hasn't really dropped that much since I've been tested.

723
00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:22.380
I was tested when I was in grad school

724
00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:24.750
on a treadmill and figured it out.

725
00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:28.710
I think, back then, I think it was like maybe 198.

726
00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:31.830
And, yeah, I mean, like 15 years later,

727
00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:35.516
it's still like probably about 192 to 194.

728
00:31:35.516 --> 00:31:36.349
Wow.

729
00:31:36.349 --> 00:31:39.630
So, if you're running a hundred mile ultra

730
00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:44.370
and you're trying to keep it right at 75% of max

731
00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:46.080
for the whole time, that's like,

732
00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:48.300
what, 145 or somewhere around that, right?

733
00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:51.340
Yeah, yeah, about 145 to 150.

734
00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:54.780
But I don't wear my heart rate strap during the race.

735
00:31:54.780 --> 00:31:57.180
I just practice it in training and get a feel

736
00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:59.280
for the effort that I wanna go at.

737
00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:02.640
And then, when I do the race, it's all just effort based.

738
00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:04.860
And then, eventually, like, you know,

739
00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:07.230
80 miles into the race in my mind.

740
00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:08.947
Forget that and then you just go.

741
00:32:08.947 --> 00:32:09.780
Yeah.

742
00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:10.800
(both laughing)

743
00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:13.362
Just ditch it and just run as hard as I can, yeah.

744
00:32:13.362 --> 00:32:16.350
Do you do proper lactate testing also?

745
00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:18.240
I haven't done that, in fact...

746
00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:19.976
For a numbers geek, I'm surprised.

747
00:32:19.976 --> 00:32:21.240
I know, I know.

748
00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:23.550
But, funny enough, like, my research

749
00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:25.950
that I did as an undergrad was blood lactate

750
00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:28.020
testing with strength training.

751
00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:31.890
And so, I got familiar with blood lactate

752
00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:33.300
testing with strength training.

753
00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:35.700
I've actually never done it on myself,

754
00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:39.330
but when I do my heart rate based progression runs,

755
00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:42.360
I push it up to the point where I start to feel my blood

756
00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:45.717
lactate and know what my heart rate is for that.

757
00:32:45.717 --> 00:32:49.680
And then, I train so that my heart rate is just below that,

758
00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:52.920
so that, you know, I'm training my blood lactate threshold.

759
00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:54.540
Right, so you have this massive

760
00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:57.583
aerobic capacity where you can train

761
00:32:57.583 --> 00:33:01.434
and race at 75 to 80% of max heart rate

762
00:33:01.434 --> 00:33:05.400
without creeping into that anaerobic zone.

763
00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:06.768
But it would be cool, though.

764
00:33:06.768 --> 00:33:09.270
It would be amazing to look at that graph

765
00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:13.170
and to see where it is exactly and then track it over time.

766
00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:14.820
Because I think what's really interesting about you,

767
00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.594
now being 40 and being this master's athlete

768
00:33:17.594 --> 00:33:20.160
and somebody who's entered the sport

769
00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:23.280
with a huge amount of speed, obviously,

770
00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:26.220
as we get older, the speed becomes harder to hold onto.

771
00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:28.740
And that's like your secret weapon right now.

772
00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:30.180
So, it's like double down on that.

773
00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:33.240
How can I leverage this speed and, you know,

774
00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.460
blow the lid on all these world records?

775
00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:37.800
But, at the same time, what's exciting is that

776
00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:40.890
as that speed will naturally wither with age

777
00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:43.500
just no matter what you do, you have this

778
00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:46.530
whole realm of continuing to be able to develop

779
00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:49.507
your aerobic capacity as an endurance athlete, right?

780
00:33:49.507 --> 00:33:50.580
Yeah, yeah.

781
00:33:50.580 --> 00:33:52.350
Which is sort of like with you doing

782
00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:54.360
the shorter stuff and not doing the longer stuff,

783
00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:56.790
I feel like there's a lot of room to grow there

784
00:33:56.790 --> 00:34:00.420
that will serve you well as you kind of continue along.

785
00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:02.670
Yeah, well, I feel like definitely

786
00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:05.639
I'm physically stronger since I've gotten older.

787
00:34:05.639 --> 00:34:09.300
And I know that because when I start to do,

788
00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:12.060
like, these progression runs, you know,

789
00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:13.920
starting at like my a hundred mile effort,

790
00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:17.340
like 75% working up to 80, 90%,

791
00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:20.070
my pace is faster and it feels easier.

792
00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:22.560
And I, like, the things I'm doing now,

793
00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:24.360
I couldn't do five years ago.

794
00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:26.070
I've just got this strength,

795
00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:28.650
this physical strength that I didn't have.

796
00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:30.880
And so, I feel like maybe what I had,

797
00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:33.684
there's kind of been this shift in, like,

798
00:34:33.684 --> 00:34:35.190
you know, maybe I was faster, you know,

799
00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:37.320
10 years ago, but I didn't have the strength

800
00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:39.330
and now I'm kind of like more strength

801
00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:41.288
and able to hold that speed.

802
00:34:41.288 --> 00:34:42.207
Right.

803
00:34:42.207 --> 00:34:43.830
So, it's really interesting.

804
00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:46.170
I feel like I'm like in my prime right now.

805
00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:48.240
I've, like, got this really good balance

806
00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:51.720
for both speed and strength and it's really more

807
00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:55.020
my strength that is allowing me to do amazing things.

808
00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.690
Right, and you have been doing more strength work, right?

809
00:34:57.690 --> 00:34:59.520
Like, I know you got like a squat rack

810
00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:01.440
and you are kind of focused on that

811
00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:03.240
in a way that you didn't used to.

812
00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:06.330
Yeah, yeah, we bought a squat rack a year ago

813
00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:10.740
and, I mean, I'd always worked like my upper body

814
00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:12.630
and I'd always just let the training,

815
00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.947
like, take care of my lower body.

816
00:35:14.947 --> 00:35:18.660
But I was in a car accident three years ago

817
00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:22.080
and I injured my back and I've had a lot

818
00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:24.812
of breakdown on the right side of my body

819
00:35:24.812 --> 00:35:27.510
and I've had to drop from races

820
00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:30.060
because my body was breaking down.

821
00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:33.630
And I finally said, you know, I need to work on this.

822
00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:36.000
I need to work on whole body strength.

823
00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:37.890
And so, we bought the squat rack

824
00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:41.310
and working my back has made such a difference.

825
00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:43.770
Like, it's just kind of unraveled all these kinks

826
00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:46.153
that I had developed on the right side of my body.

827
00:35:46.153 --> 00:35:48.000
And I feel that when I race.

828
00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:51.000
Especially, like, later when I'm running a hundred miles,

829
00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.635
I feel it later in the race, like, I feel like my form

830
00:35:53.635 --> 00:35:54.468
Stable.

831
00:35:54.468 --> 00:35:56.100
And my posture is a lot better.

832
00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:58.170
In addition to squats, what other

833
00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:01.133
dry land strength stuff do you do?

834
00:36:01.133 --> 00:36:04.050
Gosh, yeah, I mean, we've got a gym

835
00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:05.670
and I have, like, a bench.

836
00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:07.830
And I've really kind of, I mean, I've gone

837
00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:09.810
through different protocols over the years

838
00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.400
of just either doing upper body

839
00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:14.130
or some combo of upper body and lower body.

840
00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:16.020
And, really, I've just got it down

841
00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.570
to four main exercises that I do.

842
00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:22.395
And so, I do the bench press and pushups

843
00:36:22.395 --> 00:36:25.145
and squats and dead lifts and that's it.

844
00:36:25.145 --> 00:36:26.097
That's it.

845
00:36:26.097 --> 00:36:26.930
Yeah.

846
00:36:26.930 --> 00:36:27.763
That's cool.

847
00:36:27.763 --> 00:36:28.596
Yeah.

848
00:36:28.596 --> 00:36:29.429
But it seems to be working, right?

849
00:36:29.429 --> 00:36:30.262
Absolutely.

850
00:36:30.262 --> 00:36:34.320
And I love this idea of racing at 75% of max heart rate.

851
00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:35.520
Like, it's so simple.

852
00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:36.485
Yeah.

853
00:36:36.485 --> 00:36:38.220
And that just, then, dictates your strategy.

854
00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:42.210
So, you mentioned earlier at Jackpot and these other races,

855
00:36:42.210 --> 00:36:44.633
like, the men dart off the starting line,

856
00:36:44.633 --> 00:36:49.018
and it's easy to fall into, like,

857
00:36:49.018 --> 00:36:51.690
forgetting about your race strategy.

858
00:36:51.690 --> 00:36:54.240
Oh my God, they're getting away, I need to like match pace.

859
00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:56.228
But you're like, nope, this is what I'm doing.

860
00:36:56.228 --> 00:36:57.061
Yep.

861
00:36:57.061 --> 00:36:58.410
This is a long day, right?

862
00:36:58.410 --> 00:36:59.481
Yeah.

863
00:36:59.481 --> 00:37:02.040
And then, you know, come eight hours into it or whatever,

864
00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:04.530
you start to, like, you know, oh, there they are.

865
00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:06.690
They're falling apart and I'm still good.

866
00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:08.670
Just stick to what I'm doing.

867
00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:10.470
Because, in those races, you're not

868
00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:11.910
really racing anyone else.

869
00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:12.750
No.

870
00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:14.259
You're just racing yourself, right?

871
00:37:14.259 --> 00:37:15.092
Yeah.

872
00:37:15.092 --> 00:37:17.070
And to have a clear strategy that works for you

873
00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:19.740
and the confidence to maintain it,

874
00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:21.540
even when there's all this other stuff

875
00:37:21.540 --> 00:37:23.490
happening around you that you can't control,

876
00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:24.870
I think is really powerful.

877
00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:26.008
Totally.

878
00:37:26.008 --> 00:37:30.330
I just think I have a bit of maturity in the sport

879
00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:32.170
that I feel like I'm a veteran now

880
00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:35.250
and that I can be more confident in,

881
00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:36.780
you know, pulling back the reins

882
00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:38.760
earlier in the race and trusting that

883
00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:41.700
I can sustain it better later in the race.

884
00:37:41.700 --> 00:37:45.450
And so, I think it's just taken me time to get that feeling

885
00:37:45.450 --> 00:37:48.103
and to know that I don't have to go out with the men.

886
00:37:48.103 --> 00:37:50.340
That I can pull it back and, you know,

887
00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:53.176
kind of work more with my natural physiology.

888
00:37:53.176 --> 00:37:55.882
And it's been working really, really well.

889
00:37:55.882 --> 00:37:58.020
And even, you know, on the trails.

890
00:37:58.020 --> 00:38:00.030
And, yeah, everybody's like, well,

891
00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:02.400
how do you translate that effort to the trails?

892
00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:04.110
And I feel like I have such a good sense

893
00:38:04.110 --> 00:38:07.140
of what 75% effort feels like that,

894
00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:10.560
even when I get on the trails, I can channel that effort.

895
00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:13.020
And, yeah, and I just have, you know,

896
00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:15.060
that confidence that later in the race

897
00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:17.411
I'm gonna, you know, start to gain on the men.

898
00:38:17.411 --> 00:38:20.520
Yeah, well, you've been killing it on the track

899
00:38:20.520 --> 00:38:23.097
and on the road, and I think you would agree,

900
00:38:23.097 --> 00:38:26.831
like, you've excelled in these road ultras

901
00:38:26.831 --> 00:38:29.970
and in races that are relatively flat.

902
00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:31.830
I mean, Jackpot had 3,000 feet

903
00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:33.810
of elevation gain and you broke the world record.

904
00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:36.245
Like, what would've happened on a totally flat track?

905
00:38:36.245 --> 00:38:37.078
Yeah.

906
00:38:37.078 --> 00:38:38.834
I guess we'll find out 'cause you'll,

907
00:38:38.834 --> 00:38:41.110
no doubt, do that at some point.

908
00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:43.447
But trails is sort of a new

909
00:38:43.447 --> 00:38:46.044
terrain for you to conquer, right?

910
00:38:46.044 --> 00:38:46.877
Yeah.

911
00:38:46.877 --> 00:38:50.400
A lot of elevation gain, a lot of technical running.

912
00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:53.430
Like, how are you reconfiguring your training,

913
00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:56.790
or maybe you're not, to figure out that equation?

914
00:38:56.790 --> 00:38:59.250
Yeah, well, I found that I tend to do

915
00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:02.100
better on the trails if I'm really more

916
00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:05.667
focused on road to get my turnover going.

917
00:39:05.667 --> 00:39:07.770
Even running this morning, I mean,

918
00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:09.420
I got on trails with a lot of, like,

919
00:39:09.420 --> 00:39:12.000
climbing and descent and I was like, whoa.

920
00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:17.000
I mean, I grew up, like just to kind of give the analogy,

921
00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:18.720
I grew up playing the french horn

922
00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:21.180
and we used to buzz into the mouth piece.

923
00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:23.850
And once you'd put the mouth piece on the instrument,

924
00:39:23.850 --> 00:39:25.860
you're just like, woo, like just kind

925
00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:27.240
of like singing on the instrument.

926
00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:29.040
And that's kind of what it feels like

927
00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:31.287
when I really channel a lot of my road training

928
00:39:31.287 --> 00:39:33.580
and my leg speed and then bring it to the trails.

929
00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:37.033
It's just like I'm flying uphill and downhill.

930
00:39:37.033 --> 00:39:39.404
And I think that, really, the key for me

931
00:39:39.404 --> 00:39:41.520
that I found when I have done, like,

932
00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:44.280
kind of more technical trail races is I just have to,

933
00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:47.527
like, practice and get that technical skill.

934
00:39:47.527 --> 00:39:50.490
And I've been really working on that the past year.

935
00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:54.060
We lived in Arizona for a while and I was running

936
00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:55.920
on more like technical rocky stuff.

937
00:39:55.920 --> 00:39:58.170
And I feel like I kind of gained a better sense

938
00:39:58.170 --> 00:40:01.980
of how to control my body on more technical terrain.

939
00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:03.880
And it's actually becoming more fun.

940
00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:06.990
Like, I think, like, maybe five, six years ago

941
00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:09.780
when I got into trail running, it was kind of intimidating.

942
00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:11.070
And, now, I feel like I'm kind of,

943
00:40:11.070 --> 00:40:13.290
like, getting that sense of how to control

944
00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.247
the movement of my body on technical terrain.

945
00:40:16.247 --> 00:40:18.840
And so, yeah, I mean, obviously I've been

946
00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:20.967
more focused on road and track stuff,

947
00:40:20.967 --> 00:40:23.580
but I'm really, really excited to get into more

948
00:40:23.580 --> 00:40:25.680
trail running and just see what I can do.

949
00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:27.690
Yeah, I think the strength training

950
00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:29.700
probably is very helpful in that,

951
00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:31.350
like, sort of body stability

952
00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:33.423
'cause now you're kind of all over the place.

953
00:40:33.423 --> 00:40:34.282
Yeah.

954
00:40:34.282 --> 00:40:35.370
I mean, when you're on the road or the track,

955
00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:37.460
it's like a metronome, right?

956
00:40:37.460 --> 00:40:38.293
Yeah.

957
00:40:38.293 --> 00:40:39.540
Like, you just get into your pace and you just do

958
00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:41.940
your thing and you're doing these loops or whatever,

959
00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:43.830
and it becomes this mental game.

960
00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:45.990
But it's very steady state, right?

961
00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:49.140
Whereas, in a trail, even when it's an ultra,

962
00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:50.910
like, you're up, you're down,

963
00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:52.410
you're flat, you're descending.

964
00:40:52.410 --> 00:40:54.180
Like, your heart rate isn't gonna be,

965
00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:57.150
like, completely in the same place every time.

966
00:40:57.150 --> 00:40:57.983
Yeah.

967
00:40:57.983 --> 00:41:00.409
So, it's about training your body to be able

968
00:41:00.409 --> 00:41:03.139
to come back to baseline more quickly or be able

969
00:41:03.139 --> 00:41:06.540
to bounce back and adapt to all of those different inputs.

970
00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:08.880
Yeah, I think it's fun.

971
00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:12.150
Even, I mean, I went and ran Javelina last fall

972
00:41:12.150 --> 00:41:14.400
and getting back on trail and, you know,

973
00:41:14.400 --> 00:41:17.430
just flowing with that terrain and the technicality.

974
00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:20.040
I feel like I'm gaining a better sense of how to,

975
00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:23.043
I guess, like throttle my gears, like a bike.

976
00:41:23.043 --> 00:41:26.100
You know, knowing when to pull back and when to push.

977
00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:28.140
And it's fun for me.

978
00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:30.540
I feel like, like you said, I mean,

979
00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:32.940
I'm used to the road and track stuff being a metronome,

980
00:41:32.940 --> 00:41:35.340
but I get on trail and it's kind of this,

981
00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:37.290
like, you know, throttling your gears

982
00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:39.570
and learning how to move with the terrain.

983
00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:42.673
And it's fun, it's beautiful.

984
00:41:42.673 --> 00:41:44.880
I really like the camaraderie out there.

985
00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:47.250
Like just, you know, going through the aid stations

986
00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:49.170
and seeing people and they're cheering me on,

987
00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:51.000
and like that really amps you up.

988
00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:51.833
Yeah, yeah.

989
00:41:51.833 --> 00:41:54.660
Are you thinking who is your competition at Western States

990
00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:56.070
or do you not even think about that?

991
00:41:56.070 --> 00:41:58.230
You're just thinking about what you need to do for you.

992
00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:00.720
Yeah, I think I'm really somebody

993
00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:03.150
that focuses internally on my own effort.

994
00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:06.630
And, yeah, I mean, I think it's been working really well,

995
00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:09.420
that I've been more channeling my own internal effort

996
00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:12.600
and then, you know, maybe catching people later in the race.

997
00:42:12.600 --> 00:42:15.540
And, I mean, it takes confidence to be able to do that.

998
00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:17.280
You know, to be able to say that, okay,

999
00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:20.250
maybe I need to hold back earlier in the race

1000
00:42:20.250 --> 00:42:22.050
to be stronger later in the race.

1001
00:42:22.050 --> 00:42:25.020
And, yeah, I mean, I think everybody's kind of known

1002
00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:27.390
me as somebody that goes out really hard.

1003
00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:29.700
And, obviously, you know, I work

1004
00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:31.860
at a different pace than most people.

1005
00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:34.800
So, even like me looking like I'm going out hard,

1006
00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:36.900
I might be still pulling it back, you know.

1007
00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:39.960
In my mind, I'm running controlled and, you know,

1008
00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:42.960
waiting for 80 miles or later in the race to,

1009
00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:44.733
you know, really just kick it in.

1010
00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:48.420
How do you think about the gender gap?

1011
00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:51.870
Because in ultra running, maybe more than

1012
00:42:51.870 --> 00:42:54.900
almost any other sport, we're seeing the women

1013
00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:57.960
excel at a level in terms in comparison,

1014
00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:00.535
like in this ratio, to men's performances

1015
00:43:00.535 --> 00:43:03.330
that I think is super fascinating.

1016
00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:06.660
Like, the endurance capacity of the female body,

1017
00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:11.660
particularly as we get older, the proof is in the pudding.

1018
00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:15.270
Like, you're matching stride for stride with the guys,

