1519
01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:39.690
And here I am crawling on the floor and Conor's like,

1520
01:04:39.690 --> 01:04:41.537
do you think you should go run a marathon?

1521
01:04:41.537 --> 01:04:44.238
(both laughing)

1522
01:04:44.238 --> 01:04:47.010
And, yeah, I mean, like, he's the only one

1523
01:04:47.010 --> 01:04:50.520
that really understands my psyche and he knows to,

1524
01:04:50.520 --> 01:04:53.067
like, pull back the reins on me, like when

1525
01:04:53.067 --> 01:04:57.720
I'm obviously exhausted or injured or complaining.

1526
01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:01.410
I'm somebody that doesn't really complain about body issues.

1527
01:05:01.410 --> 01:05:03.690
And so, if I do complain about something,

1528
01:05:03.690 --> 01:05:04.980
that's like a red flag for him.

1529
01:05:04.980 --> 01:05:06.453
He knows it's bad, yeah.

1530
01:05:06.453 --> 01:05:07.286
Yeah.

1531
01:05:07.286 --> 01:05:10.080
And how much of it is your intuition

1532
01:05:10.080 --> 01:05:11.670
versus a prescription?

1533
01:05:11.670 --> 01:05:13.620
Like, okay, this week, these are the workouts

1534
01:05:13.620 --> 01:05:15.330
we're doing or today we're doing this.

1535
01:05:15.330 --> 01:05:17.100
And you're like, yeah, but I just want to go run

1536
01:05:17.100 --> 01:05:18.804
a marathon at midnight or whatever it is.

1537
01:05:18.804 --> 01:05:19.795
(Camille laughing)

1538
01:05:19.795 --> 01:05:21.510
Like, what is the push pull?

1539
01:05:21.510 --> 01:05:24.270
Like, how locked in is it, or does it just change

1540
01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:26.040
and it it's very much, like, day to day?

1541
01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:29.430
Like, okay, here's what I think you should do today.

1542
01:05:29.430 --> 01:05:32.730
Or you look a little tired today, maybe let's back off.

1543
01:05:32.730 --> 01:05:36.090
And when Conor says, this is what you need to do,

1544
01:05:36.090 --> 01:05:38.400
is that what you end up doing, or do you,

1545
01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:40.950
like, say, yeah, but I feel like doing this.

1546
01:05:40.950 --> 01:05:42.750
Like, what does that tension

1547
01:05:42.750 --> 01:05:44.940
or relationship or communication look like?

1548
01:05:44.940 --> 01:05:47.940
Yeah, so, when he first started coaching me,

1549
01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:52.940
like 15, was it 2004, 18 years ago or so,

1550
01:05:53.010 --> 01:05:55.830
he used to just tell me what to do and I just did it

1551
01:05:55.830 --> 01:05:58.500
without even thinking about what I was doing.

1552
01:05:58.500 --> 01:06:02.730
And so, about 2009, he started, like, kind of letting

1553
01:06:02.730 --> 01:06:05.880
me have feedback on what I felt like doing.

1554
01:06:05.880 --> 01:06:09.180
And so, that was when the magic started to come out.

1555
01:06:09.180 --> 01:06:11.610
I felt like when I started to tune in with,

1556
01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:13.050
like, what do I feel like doing,

1557
01:06:13.050 --> 01:06:15.750
what does my body and my mind feel like doing,

1558
01:06:15.750 --> 01:06:20.214
like, that was, like, what elevated me to whole other level.

1559
01:06:20.214 --> 01:06:23.880
And so, I think we kind of have that day to day dynamic

1560
01:06:23.880 --> 01:06:26.190
that we kind of assess like how I'm feeling,

1561
01:06:26.190 --> 01:06:28.440
what the weather's like and, you know,

1562
01:06:28.440 --> 01:06:31.260
sometimes I have, like, other commitments for the day

1563
01:06:31.260 --> 01:06:33.000
and, like, maybe I had a workout planned

1564
01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:35.423
and, you know, just based on, like,

1565
01:06:35.423 --> 01:06:37.380
kind of all those factors, you know,

1566
01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:39.300
I might move a workout to a different day

1567
01:06:39.300 --> 01:06:41.910
or, like, alter the workout.

1568
01:06:41.910 --> 01:06:44.970
So, I feel like the only thing that's really changed,

1569
01:06:44.970 --> 01:06:47.100
maybe, as I've gotten older is I'm kind of more

1570
01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:50.910
in tune with, sometimes I don't do a hard workout.

1571
01:06:50.910 --> 01:06:53.790
Like, maybe I'll only do one hard workout a week.

1572
01:06:53.790 --> 01:06:56.130
Like, I'm kind of working more with my body.

1573
01:06:56.130 --> 01:06:57.510
And rather than being strict,

1574
01:06:57.510 --> 01:06:59.730
I'm like having two hard workouts a week,

1575
01:06:59.730 --> 01:07:01.260
sometimes I only do one workout

1576
01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:03.090
a week if I'm feeling more tired.

1577
01:07:03.090 --> 01:07:05.310
So, I feel like I'm kind of more in tune with my body

1578
01:07:05.310 --> 01:07:08.606
and not feeling like I have to push it all the time.

1579
01:07:08.606 --> 01:07:11.850
That I just really wanna feel good, I wanna be healthy.

1580
01:07:11.850 --> 01:07:14.310
I wanna, you know, toe the line for race,

1581
01:07:14.310 --> 01:07:17.653
like feeling like really energized and really good.

1582
01:07:17.653 --> 01:07:19.590
And you mentioned spreadsheets,

1583
01:07:19.590 --> 01:07:21.510
so are you still keeping spreadsheets

1584
01:07:21.510 --> 01:07:24.510
and what can be found on those spreadsheets?

1585
01:07:24.510 --> 01:07:26.340
Like, what are you tracking specifically

1586
01:07:26.340 --> 01:07:28.230
that you're interested in watching?

1587
01:07:28.230 --> 01:07:31.860
Yeah, well, I keep Strava, so most of my training

1588
01:07:31.860 --> 01:07:35.310
is, like, kept on Strava, but for the spreadsheets,

1589
01:07:35.310 --> 01:07:38.970
I just keep track of my mileage and any sort of injuries

1590
01:07:38.970 --> 01:07:41.358
or problems I've had through the year.

1591
01:07:41.358 --> 01:07:44.310
So, it's not too much detail on the spreadsheets.

1592
01:07:44.310 --> 01:07:46.320
I keep it pretty minimal.

1593
01:07:46.320 --> 01:07:49.380
But it's incredible to look at because, I mean,

1594
01:07:49.380 --> 01:07:51.630
I'm going back, you know, to the seventh grade

1595
01:07:51.630 --> 01:07:53.700
and I've kept all these, like, details

1596
01:07:53.700 --> 01:07:57.060
of, like, body issues and changes, you know,

1597
01:07:57.060 --> 01:07:58.860
going from school to work.

1598
01:07:58.860 --> 01:08:01.230
And, yeah, I mean, just it's this

1599
01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:03.611
whole evolution of my running career.

1600
01:08:03.611 --> 01:08:06.038
But, yeah, I mean, thank goodness for Strava.

1601
01:08:06.038 --> 01:08:06.871
(Camille laughing)

1602
01:08:06.871 --> 01:08:10.080
Do you use training peaks or any other kind of database

1603
01:08:10.080 --> 01:08:12.190
management or you just use Strava?

1604
01:08:12.190 --> 01:08:14.490
Yeah, I mean, I've got my COROS watch

1605
01:08:14.490 --> 01:08:17.010
which keeps a lot of stats and, you know,

1606
01:08:17.010 --> 01:08:20.040
sleep and those sort of things as well.

1607
01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:22.140
So, I kind of look at what COROS

1608
01:08:22.140 --> 01:08:24.343
is tracking along with Strava.

1609
01:08:25.290 --> 01:08:27.750
But, yeah, I mean, it's pretty incredible.

1610
01:08:27.750 --> 01:08:30.840
Like, there's so much data that, you know, is out there now,

1611
01:08:30.840 --> 01:08:32.940
and I don't even know how to digest all of it.

1612
01:08:32.940 --> 01:08:34.980
But, at the same time, I am a bit of a data geek.

1613
01:08:34.980 --> 01:08:36.000
Yeah, you're the math person.

1614
01:08:36.000 --> 01:08:37.182
Yeah.

1615
01:08:37.182 --> 01:08:38.490
(Camille laughing)

1616
01:08:38.490 --> 01:08:41.133
Let's talk about the mindset side of things.

1617
01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:45.930
Obviously, when you're going a hundred miles or 24 hours,

1618
01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:47.850
everybody wants to know, you know,

1619
01:08:47.850 --> 01:08:49.410
what are you thinking the whole time,

1620
01:08:49.410 --> 01:08:52.140
or when you feel like quitting, what do you tell yourself?

1621
01:08:52.140 --> 01:08:54.720
Like, how does all of that work for you?

1622
01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:56.490
Yeah, gosh, I mean, there's a lot

1623
01:08:56.490 --> 01:08:59.280
of time to think when you're out there.

1624
01:08:59.280 --> 01:09:02.393
I'm somebody that, like I said, I train, you know,

1625
01:09:02.393 --> 01:09:05.545
for 75% of effort for a hundred miles.

1626
01:09:05.545 --> 01:09:09.780
For 24 hours, I'm training for 65 to 70% effort,

1627
01:09:09.780 --> 01:09:11.760
which is like an easy run pace.

1628
01:09:11.760 --> 01:09:13.830
So, I'm really just channeling my training

1629
01:09:13.830 --> 01:09:17.999
and, like, how I felt in my training running at 75%,

1630
01:09:17.999 --> 01:09:21.660
running at 65%, and I really just focus on that

1631
01:09:21.660 --> 01:09:23.910
and that feeling and that effort.

1632
01:09:23.910 --> 01:09:27.240
And, obviously, I'm somebody that,

1633
01:09:27.240 --> 01:09:29.280
like, is able to tune out pain

1634
01:09:29.280 --> 01:09:31.830
and to tune out the fatigue that I'm feeling.

1635
01:09:31.830 --> 01:09:34.350
But I'm also able to tune in to the things

1636
01:09:34.350 --> 01:09:36.720
when I need to tune in, you know,

1637
01:09:36.720 --> 01:09:39.780
keeping track of, like, how did my feet feel?

1638
01:09:39.780 --> 01:09:41.400
And, you know, how's my gut feel.

1639
01:09:41.400 --> 01:09:43.860
Like, do I need to change anything with my energy?

1640
01:09:43.860 --> 01:09:46.890
So, I'm somebody that kind of like,

1641
01:09:46.890 --> 01:09:48.750
I feel like I'm out there just kind of like

1642
01:09:48.750 --> 01:09:51.120
meditating a bit, but I'm able

1643
01:09:51.120 --> 01:09:53.460
to tune in when I need to tune in

1644
01:09:53.460 --> 01:09:56.910
and, you know, make changes when I need to make changes.

1645
01:09:56.910 --> 01:09:59.190
Are there any mantras or things that

1646
01:09:59.190 --> 01:10:02.400
you tell yourself when it is getting really painful

1647
01:10:02.400 --> 01:10:04.770
and you're in that pain cave dark place?

1648
01:10:04.770 --> 01:10:06.390
Yeah, oh definitely.

1649
01:10:06.390 --> 01:10:11.390
I mean, one of the funny things when I was running

1650
01:10:11.490 --> 01:10:13.740
my a hundred mile world record and,

1651
01:10:13.740 --> 01:10:16.290
I mean, the sun is like baking on us.

1652
01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:19.110
I felt like it was like being like ant

1653
01:10:19.110 --> 01:10:22.860
with a magnify glass on us and sitting in this bowl.

1654
01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:25.290
And probably just after 50 miles,

1655
01:10:25.290 --> 01:10:27.345
I was telling myself to suck it up, Buttercup.

1656
01:10:27.345 --> 01:10:29.820
(Camille laughing)

1657
01:10:29.820 --> 01:10:31.876
Pretty straightforward, right?

1658
01:10:31.876 --> 01:10:33.570
Yeah, suck it up, buttercup.

1659
01:10:33.570 --> 01:10:34.740
I mean, that's one of my favorite.

1660
01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:35.940
I'm kind of somebody that's like

1661
01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:38.310
a tough love type of person.

1662
01:10:38.310 --> 01:10:41.766
And, yeah, I mean, I'm out there

1663
01:10:41.766 --> 01:10:44.296
and there's certain things like,

1664
01:10:44.296 --> 01:10:47.340
I have this mentality that I'm an animal out there

1665
01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:50.438
and I'm chasing prey or I'm being chased by an animal.

1666
01:10:50.438 --> 01:10:52.080
Which animal are you?

1667
01:10:52.080 --> 01:10:53.108
Which animal?

1668
01:10:53.108 --> 01:10:54.450
The honey badger, like what is it,

1669
01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:56.460
what specific animal are you?

1670
01:10:56.460 --> 01:11:00.390
I saw, yeah, I saw your interview with Harvey.

1671
01:11:00.390 --> 01:11:03.032
I think he said he was channeling the honey badger.

1672
01:11:03.032 --> 01:11:04.950
And, I mean, I think I'm a lion.

1673
01:11:04.950 --> 01:11:07.620
I think I'm a lion, especially with my hair.

1674
01:11:07.620 --> 01:11:09.690
And you always wear your hair down, don't you?

1675
01:11:09.690 --> 01:11:10.890
Yeah, I do, I do.

1676
01:11:10.890 --> 01:11:12.990
And I feel like I literally feel

1677
01:11:12.990 --> 01:11:14.910
like I have a cape on my head when I run.

1678
01:11:14.910 --> 01:11:16.950
Like, it feels like I'm flying out there

1679
01:11:16.950 --> 01:11:20.670
and like, my hair just, like, kind of stimulates me

1680
01:11:20.670 --> 01:11:24.120
in such a way that I feel, like, free and liberated.

1681
01:11:24.120 --> 01:11:26.010
But I feel like I'm an animal out there,

1682
01:11:26.010 --> 01:11:29.010
like chasing prey and, I mean, that's like

1683
01:11:29.010 --> 01:11:30.606
the mindset that I have even like

1684
01:11:30.606 --> 01:11:33.237
racing Harvey here last weekend.

1685
01:11:33.237 --> 01:11:35.850
I felt like I was being chased by an animal

1686
01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:39.521
and I better, like, I better survive, like, yeah.

1687
01:11:39.521 --> 01:11:41.850
Well, I have this theory, I mean, when I look

1688
01:11:41.850 --> 01:11:45.150
at you and I'm experiencing you, you're clearly

1689
01:11:45.150 --> 01:11:48.210
a very happy person, a very joyful person.

1690
01:11:48.210 --> 01:11:51.510
And, again, it's impossible to not compare you

1691
01:11:51.510 --> 01:11:56.510
to Courtney's disposition, also a very happy joyful person.

1692
01:11:56.820 --> 01:11:58.860
And even like Harvey, Harvey's like a, you know,

1693
01:11:58.860 --> 01:12:01.800
he's like overflowing with like gratitude.

1694
01:12:01.800 --> 01:12:05.070
And I think it's curious that some of the most successful

1695
01:12:05.070 --> 01:12:08.190
ultra runners in the world have this disposition.

1696
01:12:08.190 --> 01:12:10.320
And I have to think that there's something to that.

1697
01:12:10.320 --> 01:12:12.360
Like, what is the relationship?

1698
01:12:12.360 --> 01:12:15.420
Like, if you're a really happy joyful person,

1699
01:12:15.420 --> 01:12:17.040
on some level what you're doing

1700
01:12:17.040 --> 01:12:18.720
is in pursuit of that joy, right?

1701
01:12:18.720 --> 01:12:22.350
So when it gets really hard, like, you have that, right?

1702
01:12:22.350 --> 01:12:27.350
And that is in contrast to this idea that

1703
01:12:27.420 --> 01:12:30.510
I think is out there that ultra running is hard

1704
01:12:30.510 --> 01:12:32.370
and you gotta be tough and it's sort of this

1705
01:12:32.370 --> 01:12:36.840
alpha Navy Seal attitude of like tackling obstacles

1706
01:12:36.840 --> 01:12:39.436
and all of that, because it's like

1707
01:12:39.436 --> 01:12:41.730
you're holding it a little bit lighter, right?

1708
01:12:41.730 --> 01:12:43.020
Like, oh, this is fun.

1709
01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:45.330
And like, let's remember that this is fun,

1710
01:12:45.330 --> 01:12:47.250
you know, when it's really dark.

1711
01:12:47.250 --> 01:12:50.130
And I think there's a real strength there.

1712
01:12:50.130 --> 01:12:53.400
It would be interesting to, like, study all the elite

1713
01:12:53.400 --> 01:12:57.060
ultra runners from that perspective of joy, happiness,

1714
01:12:57.060 --> 01:12:59.730
gratitude, because I see it everywhere I look.

1715
01:12:59.730 --> 01:13:03.210
Yeah, I think I got it from my parents.

1716
01:13:03.210 --> 01:13:05.820
I've seen my parents have their own health issues

1717
01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:08.010
and struggles that they go through.

1718
01:13:08.010 --> 01:13:10.770
I think about my mom, she had like shoulder surgery

1719
01:13:10.770 --> 01:13:13.590
and she had to go through PT for many months

1720
01:13:13.590 --> 01:13:16.170
and I've never seen my mom, like, I mean,

1721
01:13:16.170 --> 01:13:19.590
my mom is like this, you know, Betty Crocker type that,

1722
01:13:19.590 --> 01:13:22.860
you know, like cooks and, you know, she's at home

1723
01:13:22.860 --> 01:13:25.800
and she's like, you know, she goes hardcore.

1724
01:13:25.800 --> 01:13:29.550
She goes hardcore with her PT and she's so positive.

1725
01:13:29.550 --> 01:13:33.060
And she, you know, she was motivated, she's positive.

1726
01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:35.490
And I think I got that from my parents.

1727
01:13:35.490 --> 01:13:37.320
I mean, growing up, playing sports

1728
01:13:37.320 --> 01:13:39.690
and my parents were just such positive people,

1729
01:13:39.690 --> 01:13:43.350
like just super motivating and, like, believed in me.

1730
01:13:43.350 --> 01:13:47.127
And I think, like, I got it from my parents, yeah.

1731
01:13:47.127 --> 01:13:50.280
And you guys lost your home, right, growing up?

1732
01:13:50.280 --> 01:13:51.113
Yeah.

1733
01:13:51.113 --> 01:13:51.946
To a tornado?

1734
01:13:51.946 --> 01:13:53.790
We did, we did.

1735
01:13:53.790 --> 01:13:56.490
Yeah, that happened my junior year of high school.

1736
01:13:56.490 --> 01:14:00.180
And it actually happened a couple days after prom.

1737
01:14:00.180 --> 01:14:05.180
And so, I'd had my prom dress hanging on my bathroom door

1738
01:14:05.306 --> 01:14:08.640
and then the tornado happened on a Monday.

1739
01:14:08.640 --> 01:14:10.890
And I remember I'd gone to lunch with my friends

1740
01:14:10.890 --> 01:14:13.620
that day and it was this perfect sunny day.

1741
01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:18.620
And then, yeah, the tornado came and my dad,

1742
01:14:19.020 --> 01:14:21.240
so my mom was down in Norman taking

1743
01:14:21.240 --> 01:14:23.160
care of my grandpa at the time.

1744
01:14:23.160 --> 01:14:26.228
And so, my dad I'd like gotten outta the shower

1745
01:14:26.228 --> 01:14:28.410
and my dad was like, hey, there's your tornado coming.

1746
01:14:28.410 --> 01:14:30.840
And, you know, we get tornadoes all the time

1747
01:14:30.840 --> 01:14:33.210
in Oklahoma, you don't think really anything of it.

1748
01:14:33.210 --> 01:14:34.453
You're just like, okay, you know,

1749
01:14:34.453 --> 01:14:37.260
I gotta go take shelter, there's a tornado coming.

1750
01:14:37.260 --> 01:14:40.620
So, I just grabbed my book bag and went with my dad

1751
01:14:40.620 --> 01:14:42.360
and we drove down to Norman

1752
01:14:42.360 --> 01:14:45.630
to be with my mom and my grandpa.

1753
01:14:45.630 --> 01:14:48.270
And I'm watching the TV and, like,

1754
01:14:48.270 --> 01:14:51.090
here's this tornado coming through Oklahoma City

1755
01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:53.580
and, you know, telling us the streets

1756
01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:56.340
that it's going through and, I mean,

1757
01:14:56.340 --> 01:14:58.773
we're like, oh my gosh, that's where we live.

1758
01:14:59.610 --> 01:15:01.800
And so, yeah, it just ripped, like,

1759
01:15:01.800 --> 01:15:04.170
right through where my high school was,

1760
01:15:04.170 --> 01:15:06.360
the neighborhood to the south.

1761
01:15:06.360 --> 01:15:08.190
It ripped, like, right where we lived,

1762
01:15:08.190 --> 01:15:11.108
which is right across the street from the high school.

1763
01:15:11.108 --> 01:15:14.790
But, yeah, it was pretty traumatizing at the time.

1764
01:15:14.790 --> 01:15:17.400
So, your whole house was gone.

1765
01:15:17.400 --> 01:15:21.000
Yeah, so we actually, so what had happened

1766
01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:24.600
was we lived down in Duncan, Oklahoma,

1767
01:15:24.600 --> 01:15:26.520
and we had just moved up to the city,

1768
01:15:26.520 --> 01:15:29.010
like, a couple months before 'cause my dad

1769
01:15:29.010 --> 01:15:32.580
had retired and we were trying to decide,

1770
01:15:32.580 --> 01:15:34.830
like, where to live in south Oklahoma City.

1771
01:15:34.830 --> 01:15:37.020
And so, we were either gonna live, like,

1772
01:15:37.020 --> 01:15:38.640
across the street from the high school

1773
01:15:38.640 --> 01:15:40.650
or we were gonna live in another neighborhood.

1774
01:15:40.650 --> 01:15:43.080
Both places got hit by the tornado.

1775
01:15:43.080 --> 01:15:46.547
And so, we ended up, we had an apartment at the time

1776
01:15:46.547 --> 01:15:48.900
'cause my parents were trying to sell their house.

1777
01:15:48.900 --> 01:15:50.640
And so, the tornado hit the apartment

1778
01:15:50.640 --> 01:15:52.842
where we were staying at.

1779
01:15:52.842 --> 01:15:56.580
And so, I mean, it just, there were like four people

1780
01:15:56.580 --> 01:15:59.850
where we lived in that vicinity that died.

1781
01:15:59.850 --> 01:16:03.360
And so, I mean, I feel so blessed that my dad

1782
01:16:03.360 --> 01:16:06.420
had loaded me up in the car to go down to Norman.

1783
01:16:06.420 --> 01:16:09.720
And, you know, when we were able to get back up there

1784
01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:13.460
and, you know, I mean, it's just concrete slabs up there.

1785
01:16:13.460 --> 01:16:16.560
And so, it was really shocking.

1786
01:16:16.560 --> 01:16:18.720
I mean, at the time you're thinking, oh my gosh,

1787
01:16:18.720 --> 01:16:21.623
like, you know, I can't believe this happened.

1788
01:16:21.623 --> 01:16:24.960
But, at the same time, like I felt so grateful for my life

1789
01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:29.327
and you know, what my dad had done, you know, to load us up.

1790
01:16:29.327 --> 01:16:32.910
And, yeah, I mean, I just felt like this

1791
01:16:32.910 --> 01:16:35.820
appreciation for, like, I'm alive.

1792
01:16:35.820 --> 01:16:39.180
Like, I can still run, I still have my running talent.

1793
01:16:39.180 --> 01:16:42.240
And it kind of, like, made me like become

1794
01:16:42.240 --> 01:16:44.617
more spiritual with myself and I felt like,

1795
01:16:44.617 --> 01:16:48.840
like I started running long on Sunday as a way

1796
01:16:48.840 --> 01:16:52.200
to, like, celebrate my life and, like, my running talent.

1797
01:16:52.200 --> 01:16:56.700
And, yeah, and that really like kind of put me back in touch

1798
01:16:56.700 --> 01:17:00.630
with myself and, like, being appreciative of my life.

1799
01:17:00.630 --> 01:17:03.065
That gratitude thing, yeah, yeah, yeah.

1800
01:17:03.065 --> 01:17:04.139
And now you're back there.

1801
01:17:04.139 --> 01:17:04.972
Yeah.

1802
01:17:04.972 --> 01:17:06.782
Like, you've kind of been nomadic, right?

1803
01:17:06.782 --> 01:17:09.450
You were in Colorado, you were in Tucson,

1804
01:17:09.450 --> 01:17:10.493
now you're back in Oklahoma City.

1805
01:17:10.493 --> 01:17:12.300
Back to Oklahoma, love it.

1806
01:17:12.300 --> 01:17:14.788
On the tornado watch.

1807
01:17:14.788 --> 01:17:15.621
Yes.

1808
01:17:15.621 --> 01:17:17.155
Has there been any since you've been back?

1809
01:17:17.155 --> 01:17:18.160
It's pretty recent, right?

1810
01:17:18.160 --> 01:17:18.996
Yeah.

1811
01:17:18.996 --> 01:17:20.229
You moved back there pretty recently.

1812
01:17:20.229 --> 01:17:22.140
Yeah, so we just moved back here a couple months ago

1813
01:17:22.140 --> 01:17:24.839
and we have a storm shelter in our house.

1814
01:17:24.839 --> 01:17:27.012
(both laughing)

1815
01:17:27.012 --> 01:17:28.620
In fact, I've joked I've thought about like...

1816
01:17:28.620 --> 01:17:29.453
This is new for Conor, right?

1817
01:17:29.453 --> 01:17:32.085
Like, not used to like tornadoes, yeah.

1818
01:17:32.085 --> 01:17:35.490
Yeah, I mean, yeah, I haven't even gotten

1819
01:17:35.490 --> 01:17:37.620
down in there yet to see what the storm shelter's

1820
01:17:37.620 --> 01:17:40.016
like, but we have one in our garage.

1821
01:17:40.016 --> 01:17:42.780
So, yeah, you gotta get underground

1822
01:17:42.780 --> 01:17:44.760
if a tornado is coming 'cause it's,

1823
01:17:44.760 --> 01:17:48.000
yeah, just completely flattens your house.

1824
01:17:48.000 --> 01:17:49.710
But, yeah, I mean, I'm from Oklahoma,

1825
01:17:49.710 --> 01:17:51.840
I'm a native Oklahoman and my parents live there.

1826
01:17:51.840 --> 01:17:54.620
My two sisters live there and I just,

1827
01:17:54.620 --> 01:17:57.630
I feel like I'm reconnecting like with my roots

1828
01:17:57.630 --> 01:17:59.790
as a runner and, you know, where I'm from.

1829
01:17:59.790 --> 01:18:00.840
Yeah, yeah.

1830
01:18:00.840 --> 01:18:03.940
So, but it's pretty flat there, right?

1831
01:18:03.940 --> 01:18:05.100
Like, you're not getting a lot

1832
01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:07.920
of hill work for Western States.

1833
01:18:07.920 --> 01:18:12.330
Yeah, so the way that I was able to train and to win

1834
01:18:12.330 --> 01:18:15.690
the Comrades marathon was I went down to Mount Scott,

1835
01:18:15.690 --> 01:18:18.672
which is like our only, like, mountain in Oklahoma.

1836
01:18:18.672 --> 01:18:21.202
(Camille laughing)

1837
01:18:21.202 --> 01:18:22.777
Like 1,500 feet high or something.

1838
01:18:22.777 --> 01:18:24.397
Yeah, I think it goes up to like

1839
01:18:24.397 --> 01:18:26.190
2,000 feet or something like that.

1840
01:18:26.190 --> 01:18:28.200
It's like just under three miles

1841
01:18:28.200 --> 01:18:30.243
to go up to about 2,000 feet.

1842
01:18:31.193 --> 01:18:33.242
It's a pretty steep grade.

1843
01:18:33.242 --> 01:18:37.140
Like, I think it goes maybe from, like, 8% up to 20% or so.

1844
01:18:37.140 --> 01:18:40.260
But, yeah, that's basically how I trained to win

1845
01:18:40.260 --> 01:18:44.280
the Comrades uphill course was driving down to Mount Scott

1846
01:18:44.280 --> 01:18:46.860
and doing repeats up and down the mountain.

1847
01:18:46.860 --> 01:18:50.070
And then I would do short repeats at the bottom as well.

1848
01:18:50.070 --> 01:18:51.900
And so, that was how I trained,

1849
01:18:51.900 --> 01:18:54.060
that was how I hill trained for Comrades.

1850
01:18:54.060 --> 01:18:57.270
And there's also trails down there to get on.

1851
01:18:57.270 --> 01:18:59.190
I really like the trails.

1852
01:18:59.190 --> 01:19:00.570
And now is that what you're doing

1853
01:19:00.570 --> 01:19:03.450
for Western States or what's the prep like?

1854
01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:04.890
Like, this is the big goal.

1855
01:19:04.890 --> 01:19:05.723
Yeah.

1856
01:19:05.723 --> 01:19:07.290
It's coming up, when is it, in July?

1857
01:19:07.290 --> 01:19:09.420
Yeah, the end of June.

1858
01:19:09.420 --> 01:19:10.470
End of June, okay.

1859
01:19:10.470 --> 01:19:12.125
Yeah.

1860
01:19:12.125 --> 01:19:13.320
So, where are we at and what are you focused on?

1861
01:19:13.320 --> 01:19:15.180
Like, this is the thing, right?

1862
01:19:15.180 --> 01:19:16.273
Yeah.

1863
01:19:16.273 --> 01:19:17.106
Like, you've got all these world records,

1864
01:19:17.106 --> 01:19:18.580
but, like, it's all about Western States.

1865
01:19:18.580 --> 01:19:20.850
Yeah, now I'm trying to bring it to the trail.

1866
01:19:20.850 --> 01:19:22.890
So, super fun.

1867
01:19:22.890 --> 01:19:26.040
I mean, I'm gonna do the Western States

1868
01:19:26.040 --> 01:19:29.351
training camp coming up here in a couple weeks,

1869
01:19:29.351 --> 01:19:31.920
which is something I haven't done before.

1870
01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:35.037
And, I mean, I ran Western States last year.

1871
01:19:35.037 --> 01:19:37.620
And so, I've kind of got like the mental picture

1872
01:19:37.620 --> 01:19:40.710
of what it's like and, you know, the different,

1873
01:19:40.710 --> 01:19:44.280
you know, you kind of have to think of it in segments

1874
01:19:44.280 --> 01:19:46.080
'cause there's so many different parts

1875
01:19:46.080 --> 01:19:48.780
and kind of how you break it down.

1876
01:19:48.780 --> 01:19:50.790
And I feel like since I've run the whole course,

1877
01:19:50.790 --> 01:19:53.070
I can, like, mentally process that better

1878
01:19:53.070 --> 01:19:55.000
and how to prepare for it.

1879
01:19:55.000 --> 01:19:57.960
But, yeah, I'm planning to go out to the training camp

1880
01:19:57.960 --> 01:19:59.640
and get on the course more and just

1881
01:19:59.640 --> 01:20:01.710
kind of get dialed in with that flow

1882
01:20:01.710 --> 01:20:05.790
and that connection of my body with the course.

1883
01:20:05.790 --> 01:20:08.280
But, yeah, I mean, you know, you kind of just try

1884
01:20:08.280 --> 01:20:10.620
and put it all together on race day

1885
01:20:10.620 --> 01:20:12.990
and you know, hope that you've like, you know,

1886
01:20:12.990 --> 01:20:14.320
dialed in all the different components.

1887
01:20:14.320 --> 01:20:15.900
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1888
01:20:15.900 --> 01:20:18.570
You seem really clear on your strengths.

1889
01:20:18.570 --> 01:20:21.690
What do you think are your weaknesses

1890
01:20:21.690 --> 01:20:25.680
or the areas that really need a little bit more focus?

1891
01:20:25.680 --> 01:20:28.230
Yeah, well, definitely one of the things

1892
01:20:28.230 --> 01:20:31.380
was my body breaking down and, like,

1893
01:20:31.380 --> 01:20:33.960
I've had to drop from races because I had

1894
01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:37.050
hamstring issues or just my body breaking down.

1895
01:20:37.050 --> 01:20:40.050
And so, I've spent, you know, the past year

1896
01:20:40.050 --> 01:20:42.030
really, like, getting in the gym and really

1897
01:20:42.030 --> 01:20:44.220
working on my full body strength

1898
01:20:44.220 --> 01:20:46.110
so that I'm not breaking down.

1899
01:20:46.110 --> 01:20:49.230
And I felt like, even last year, when I ran Western States,

1900
01:20:49.230 --> 01:20:51.240
even though I had this iron issue,

1901
01:20:51.240 --> 01:20:52.920
my body wasn't breaking down.

1902
01:20:52.920 --> 01:20:54.480
Like, I was physically strong

1903
01:20:54.480 --> 01:20:56.520
because I'd been working more in the gym.

1904
01:20:56.520 --> 01:20:59.730
And so, I felt like that was the first piece of the puzzle

1905
01:20:59.730 --> 01:21:02.130
was to get my body physically stronger

1906
01:21:02.130 --> 01:21:03.960
so I'm not breaking down in the race.

1907
01:21:03.960 --> 01:21:05.910
And then, the whole iron issue was the other

1908
01:21:05.910 --> 01:21:07.777
thing that I needed to work on.

1909
01:21:07.777 --> 01:21:11.160
I'm somebody that has the opposite problem of most people.

1910
01:21:11.160 --> 01:21:14.550
Like, you know, I'm having to give my blood away

1911
01:21:14.550 --> 01:21:17.040
rather than, you know, trying to build it up.

1912
01:21:17.040 --> 01:21:19.830
So, yeah, I mean, I've really worked on that.

1913
01:21:19.830 --> 01:21:24.097
Just trying to get my biomarkers more balanced again.

1914
01:21:24.097 --> 01:21:25.809
Yeah, but I feel like you're on top of that, right?

1915
01:21:25.809 --> 01:21:26.935
Yeah.

1916
01:21:26.935 --> 01:21:28.170
It's like, okay, that was a weakness, I've dealt

1917
01:21:28.170 --> 01:21:31.710
with that, what's the next weakness that I need to look at.

1918
01:21:31.710 --> 01:21:33.296
Yeah, yeah.

1919
01:21:33.296 --> 01:21:35.886
You're like, I'm good, I'm strong, I'm, like, ready to go.

1920
01:21:35.886 --> 01:21:36.780
I'm feeling pretty good, yeah.

1921
01:21:36.780 --> 01:21:38.818
Yeah, I mean, right now, like,

1922
01:21:38.818 --> 01:21:41.760
'cause I've been so focused on road stuff and track running,

1923
01:21:41.760 --> 01:21:44.757
like, I'm trying to translate it to the trails right now

1924
01:21:44.757 --> 01:21:49.380
and kind of get my flow dialed in to be on trails.

1925
01:21:49.380 --> 01:21:51.660
And, I mean, I feel great.

1926
01:21:51.660 --> 01:21:53.880
The past couple of weeks that I've gotten

1927
01:21:53.880 --> 01:21:56.010
on trails I'm like, man, I feel great.

1928
01:21:56.010 --> 01:21:58.827
Like, I feel like, damn, I'm like

1929
01:21:58.827 --> 01:22:00.741
flying like uphill and downhill.

1930
01:22:00.741 --> 01:22:04.282
And I feel like so much more comfortable

1931
01:22:04.282 --> 01:22:06.383
with my technical skills than I did, you know,

1932
01:22:06.383 --> 01:22:09.030
a year, a couple years ago.

1933
01:22:09.030 --> 01:22:10.710
And, yeah, obviously like, you know,

1934
01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:13.200
for me to take on like a course like UTMB

1935
01:22:13.200 --> 01:22:15.510
is gonna be like, you know, whole other element.

1936
01:22:15.510 --> 01:22:16.343
Right.

1937
01:22:16.343 --> 01:22:18.540
But I feel like I'm somebody that can adapt,

1938
01:22:18.540 --> 01:22:21.900
you know, I can get stronger, I can, you know, improve.

1939
01:22:21.900 --> 01:22:24.937
And, I mean, it's kind of like learning how to ride a bike.

1940
01:22:24.937 --> 01:22:27.630
You know, any athlete, you know,

1941
01:22:27.630 --> 01:22:29.700
that's trying to learn how to do something new,

1942
01:22:29.700 --> 01:22:32.130
like, if you do it, you know, over and over and over again,

1943
01:22:32.130 --> 01:22:33.873
eventually you're gonna get it right.

1944
01:22:33.873 --> 01:22:36.334
I'm optimistic.

1945
01:22:36.334 --> 01:22:38.280
You know, I'm optimistic that if I keep

1946
01:22:38.280 --> 01:22:40.380
practicing and I keep working at it,

1947
01:22:40.380 --> 01:22:42.690
like, I'm finally gonna get things right.

1948
01:22:42.690 --> 01:22:44.760
And how do you like to work with pacers?

1949
01:22:44.760 --> 01:22:47.970
Do you want somebody with you as much as possible or you

1950
01:22:47.970 --> 01:22:51.360
want a bump and run, just gimme the nutrition and I'm fine.

1951
01:22:51.360 --> 01:22:53.680
Or, like, how do you think about that piece?

1952
01:22:53.680 --> 01:22:57.450
Yeah, I'm not really, I don't think I want a pacer.

1953
01:22:57.450 --> 01:23:01.200
I actually, like a year and a half ago, it was interesting,

1954
01:23:01.200 --> 01:23:04.290
I ended up running a road 100K and had a pacer

1955
01:23:04.290 --> 01:23:06.690
and that was kind of my first time to have a pacer.

1956
01:23:06.690 --> 01:23:08.760
And I feel like I'm somebody that likes to get

1957
01:23:08.760 --> 01:23:12.330
into my own head that I don't like to be distracted.

1958
01:23:12.330 --> 01:23:14.490
I don't like to have a whole lot of people or,

1959
01:23:14.490 --> 01:23:16.470
like, outside, like, influence.

1960
01:23:16.470 --> 01:23:19.860
I really need to be in my own head space when I race.

1961
01:23:19.860 --> 01:23:23.010
And, I mean, that's kind of what I'm good at.

1962
01:23:23.010 --> 01:23:25.170
Like, getting in my own head when I race

1963
01:23:25.170 --> 01:23:27.390
and really, like, elevating myself.

1964
01:23:27.390 --> 01:23:29.910
And so, I'm actually not gonna have pacers.

1965
01:23:29.910 --> 01:23:33.283
Like, I feel pretty confident enough in my own self

1966
01:23:33.283 --> 01:23:35.820
that I can get in my own head space and like,

1967
01:23:35.820 --> 01:23:38.880
you know, to elevate myself like later in the race.

1968
01:23:38.880 --> 01:23:39.900
Yeah, yeah.

1969
01:23:39.900 --> 01:23:42.390
Talk a little bit about the longer term goals.

1970
01:23:42.390 --> 01:23:44.550
I mean, Western States is a short term goal.

1971
01:23:44.550 --> 01:23:49.110
Like, what's further down the horizon in terms

1972
01:23:49.110 --> 01:23:51.630
of things that you'd like to be able to accomplish?

1973
01:23:51.630 --> 01:23:54.930
Yeah, so, obviously, I wanna become more

1974
01:23:54.930 --> 01:23:57.780
of a trail runner and I'm really motivated.

1975
01:23:57.780 --> 01:24:00.810
I've been studying world records for many years now.

1976
01:24:00.810 --> 01:24:03.240
And, I mean, there's still some more world records

1977
01:24:03.240 --> 01:24:06.030
like in the multi-day stuff that I wanna get,

1978
01:24:06.030 --> 01:24:08.490
but I see myself evolving more into trail running

1979
01:24:08.490 --> 01:24:10.860
and mountain running and I wanna become

1980
01:24:10.860 --> 01:24:14.447
the first athlete to win Comrades, Western States,

1981
01:24:14.447 --> 01:24:18.747
UTMB because that's the triple crown of ultra running.

1982
01:24:18.747 --> 01:24:22.830
And so, winning Comrades is obviously the hardest one

1983
01:24:22.830 --> 01:24:24.930
because it's the most competitive race to win

1984
01:24:24.930 --> 01:24:27.540
and there's only been three Americans that have done it.

1985
01:24:27.540 --> 01:24:30.930
So, I feel like, you know, I won like the pinnacle,

1986
01:24:30.930 --> 01:24:32.631
you know, as far as Comrades.

1987
01:24:32.631 --> 01:24:35.760
But, yeah, now I've gotta bring it to Western States

1988
01:24:35.760 --> 01:24:38.940
and UTMB and yeah, it's gonna be a lot of fun

1989
01:24:38.940 --> 01:24:41.217
trying to, you know, achieve that.

1990
01:24:41.217 --> 01:24:43.590
But, yeah, I mean, I feel like

1991
01:24:43.590 --> 01:24:45.281
there's no limits on what I can do.

1992
01:24:45.281 --> 01:24:46.114
Right.

1993
01:24:46.114 --> 01:24:47.460
Like, if I set a goal for something,

1994
01:24:47.460 --> 01:24:49.470
like I'm somebody that doesn't really

1995
01:24:49.470 --> 01:24:52.123
limit myself on, like, I just go after it,

1996
01:24:52.123 --> 01:24:55.251
I just, you know, bite down and go after that goal.

1997
01:24:55.251 --> 01:24:58.356
I feel like you would really excel at Badwater.

1998
01:24:58.356 --> 01:25:00.870
Oh yeah, oh yeah, definitely.

1999
01:25:00.870 --> 01:25:03.160
I was actually signed up to do Badwater

2000
01:25:03.160 --> 01:25:05.358
two years ago and it got canceled.

2001
01:25:05.358 --> 01:25:06.473
Right.

2002
01:25:06.473 --> 01:25:09.150
And so, yeah, I've been staying with my friend Shannon,

2003
01:25:09.150 --> 01:25:11.970
who's done it like eight times and I mean,

2004
01:25:11.970 --> 01:25:14.070
she's all raving to me about it.

2005
01:25:14.070 --> 01:25:16.530
And, in fact, she actually did the double Badwater.

2006
01:25:16.530 --> 01:25:18.720
She ran Badwater and then turned

2007
01:25:18.720 --> 01:25:21.160
around and ran back to the start.

2008
01:25:21.160 --> 01:25:22.011
So I don't know.

2009
01:25:22.011 --> 01:25:23.310
Shannon, so we should just shout her out.

2010
01:25:23.310 --> 01:25:25.590
Like, Shannon Farar-Griefer she's a legend.

2011
01:25:25.590 --> 01:25:26.790
She lives in the area.

2012
01:25:26.790 --> 01:25:28.350
If you run trails around here,

2013
01:25:28.350 --> 01:25:30.210
you're inevitably gonna bump into her.

2014
01:25:30.210 --> 01:25:31.350
She's amazing.

2015
01:25:31.350 --> 01:25:32.183
Yeah.

2016
01:25:32.183 --> 01:25:34.110
And, yeah, was she the first to do the double

2017
01:25:34.110 --> 01:25:36.547
or, I can't remember, it was a while ago when she did it.

2018
01:25:36.547 --> 01:25:37.380
Yeah.

2019
01:25:37.380 --> 01:25:39.015
But it's, like, unbelievable.

2020
01:25:39.015 --> 01:25:40.980
Yeah, I mean, she was talking about how tired

2021
01:25:40.980 --> 01:25:43.020
she was when she finished and she turned

2022
01:25:43.020 --> 01:25:44.940
around and ran it all the way back.

2023
01:25:44.940 --> 01:25:45.990
I know.

2024
01:25:45.990 --> 01:25:50.970
I mean, that's so mental, like, it's incredible, yeah.

2025
01:25:50.970 --> 01:25:54.478
What about a transcontinental run?

2026
01:25:54.478 --> 01:25:55.763
Oh, absolutely, yeah.

2027
01:25:55.763 --> 01:25:58.860
I mean, I've got a long list of things I wanna do.

2028
01:25:58.860 --> 01:26:00.660
I mean, we're watching Michael Wardian

2029
01:26:00.660 --> 01:26:02.827
right now do transcon.

2030
01:26:02.827 --> 01:26:06.450
I mean, that stuff just totally blows my mind.

2031
01:26:06.450 --> 01:26:09.406
Like, I feel like I'm somebody that, I mean,

2032
01:26:09.406 --> 01:26:13.290
I find joy in running period, and to run across America,

2033
01:26:13.290 --> 01:26:15.630
I would just absolutely love it.

2034
01:26:15.630 --> 01:26:18.420
I mean, it would be something completely new

2035
01:26:18.420 --> 01:26:21.420
because you would see the entire country by foot.

2036
01:26:21.420 --> 01:26:23.190
And I think I would just love that.

2037
01:26:23.190 --> 01:26:26.010
I think I would just, like, be taking it in every day,

2038
01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:28.050
like, being in a new place and knowing,

2039
01:26:28.050 --> 01:26:30.300
you know, where my legs are taking me.

2040
01:26:30.300 --> 01:26:32.265
What's the women's record for that.

2041
01:26:32.265 --> 01:26:33.580
Do you know?

2042
01:26:33.580 --> 01:26:36.180
Yeah, I don't really know what that is.

2043
01:26:36.180 --> 01:26:39.420
I think Pete Kostelnick holds the men's record

2044
01:26:39.420 --> 01:26:43.230
and I've run a little bit further than him for 24 hours

2045
01:26:43.230 --> 01:26:46.410
so I think I would be trying to go for his record.

2046
01:26:46.410 --> 01:26:47.910
That would be unbelievable.

2047
01:26:47.910 --> 01:26:48.782
Yeah.

2048
01:26:48.782 --> 01:26:49.615
If you broke the world record

2049
01:26:49.615 --> 01:26:50.850
for men and women doing that.

2050
01:26:50.850 --> 01:26:53.149
Yeah, yeah, oh, totally.

2051
01:26:53.149 --> 01:26:53.982
Yeah.

2052
01:26:53.982 --> 01:26:54.960
Yeah.

2053
01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:57.390
And then there's all these FKT's, right?

2054
01:26:57.390 --> 01:26:59.430
Like, you could, I mean, there's no limit.

2055
01:26:59.430 --> 01:27:00.375
Yeah.

2056
01:27:00.375 --> 01:27:01.557
It's just a question of, like,

2057
01:27:01.557 --> 01:27:02.390
where you wanna place your attention.

2058
01:27:02.390 --> 01:27:05.550
And I think you've even mentioned the 3,100.

2059
01:27:05.550 --> 01:27:06.540
Yeah.

2060
01:27:06.540 --> 01:27:07.620
In Queens.
Yes.

2061
01:27:07.620 --> 01:27:09.331
Which is a wild one.

2062
01:27:09.331 --> 01:27:12.046
That, I mean, that is just so, that blows my mind.

2063
01:27:12.046 --> 01:27:13.829
(Camille laughing)

2064
01:27:13.829 --> 01:27:16.957
I mean, 3,100 miles, like around, like,

2065
01:27:16.957 --> 01:27:19.141
I think it's like a half mile block.

2066
01:27:19.141 --> 01:27:22.320
I mean, how do you stay motivated?

2067
01:27:22.320 --> 01:27:25.530
How do you find joy, you know, when it's boring?

2068
01:27:25.530 --> 01:27:28.230
And I think I'm somebody that like, I mean,

2069
01:27:28.230 --> 01:27:31.170
I like to use the energy around me when I'm in races

2070
01:27:31.170 --> 01:27:34.170
and you kind of have to break it down.

2071
01:27:34.170 --> 01:27:35.790
Like, how do you eat an elephant?

2072
01:27:35.790 --> 01:27:38.190
You eat an elephant one bite at a time.

2073
01:27:38.190 --> 01:27:41.160
And so, I've learned like when I run races,

2074
01:27:41.160 --> 01:27:43.920
like I need to, you know, track the mileage

2075
01:27:43.920 --> 01:27:45.780
and I need to break down the how

2076
01:27:45.780 --> 01:27:47.160
am I going to eat the elephant?

2077
01:27:47.160 --> 01:27:48.251
Yeah.

2078
01:27:48.251 --> 01:27:50.580
And so, I mean, that's something I think about in races.

2079
01:27:50.580 --> 01:27:53.263
Like, you know, whether I'm going 100 miles, 24 hours,

2080
01:27:53.263 --> 01:27:57.331
3,100 miles, like how do you break down eating the elephant?

2081
01:27:57.331 --> 01:27:59.990
Well, you're no stranger to running around in circles.

2082
01:27:59.990 --> 01:28:00.823
(Camille laughing)

2083
01:28:00.823 --> 01:28:02.550
So, you're running around in a circle.

2084
01:28:02.550 --> 01:28:03.810
The interesting thing with that

2085
01:28:03.810 --> 01:28:06.780
one is the course closes at night.

2086
01:28:06.780 --> 01:28:10.110
So, you go to bed and it becomes like a job for the summer.

2087
01:28:10.110 --> 01:28:13.080
You just wake up and between eight and five or whatever,

2088
01:28:13.080 --> 01:28:15.210
eight and eight or whatever the hours are,

2089
01:28:15.210 --> 01:28:16.707
you just go around that loop

2090
01:28:16.707 --> 01:28:19.290
and try to get as many miles as you can.

2091
01:28:19.290 --> 01:28:21.810
There's a documentary about it that my friend,

2092
01:28:21.810 --> 01:28:23.790
Sanjay Rawal, made, I don't know if you've seen it.

2093
01:28:23.790 --> 01:28:24.690
Yeah.

2094
01:28:24.690 --> 01:28:26.040
And it's so interesting.

2095
01:28:26.040 --> 01:28:29.400
The people that excel in that race are not the people,

2096
01:28:29.400 --> 01:28:31.200
like, they don't look like runners,

2097
01:28:31.200 --> 01:28:33.039
they're, like, average people with,

2098
01:28:33.039 --> 01:28:34.664
you know, all different kinds of jobs

2099
01:28:34.664 --> 01:28:37.320
and it really shows you like, oh,

2100
01:28:37.320 --> 01:28:39.390
this is about mental fortitude.

2101
01:28:39.390 --> 01:28:40.251
Yeah.

2102
01:28:40.251 --> 01:28:43.560
This is about like something more than athletic capacity.

2103
01:28:43.560 --> 01:28:44.700
This is about like...

2104
01:28:44.700 --> 01:28:45.533
Spiritual.

2105
01:28:45.533 --> 01:28:46.890
A connection to something beyond.

2106
01:28:46.890 --> 01:28:49.740
Like, those are the people that seem to, you know, excel.

2107
01:28:49.740 --> 01:28:51.960
Yeah. Yeah and I feel that.

2108
01:28:51.960 --> 01:28:54.180
I feel a spiritual connection.

2109
01:28:54.180 --> 01:28:56.580
When I go for world records, I feel like

2110
01:28:56.580 --> 01:29:00.420
I'm tapping into something really special inside of me.

2111
01:29:00.420 --> 01:29:02.970
And I reach a point where I, like, transcend.

2112
01:29:02.970 --> 01:29:04.410
And, like, it's almost like my head

2113
01:29:04.410 --> 01:29:06.480
starts to float off of my body.

2114
01:29:06.480 --> 01:29:09.510
And I think when you do something like that,

2115
01:29:09.510 --> 01:29:12.480
you really have to tap into that spiritual energy

2116
01:29:12.480 --> 01:29:15.887
inside of you to keep going and to complete it.

2117
01:29:15.887 --> 01:29:19.230
'Cause it's just something that just, like, you know,

2118
01:29:19.230 --> 01:29:22.590
it's just like beyond, like, what you think is possible.

2119
01:29:22.590 --> 01:29:23.927
Tell me more about that.

2120
01:29:23.927 --> 01:29:24.810
(Camille laughing)

2121
01:29:24.810 --> 01:29:28.500
Yeah, I feel like I've always had this ability

2122
01:29:28.500 --> 01:29:33.500
to tap into this extra energy inside of me.

2123
01:29:33.870 --> 01:29:36.930
And I, like, I remember when I set my 50 mile

2124
01:29:36.930 --> 01:29:39.930
world best back in 2015, I think I had,

2125
01:29:39.930 --> 01:29:43.200
like, maybe it's like 12 miles to go,

2126
01:29:43.200 --> 01:29:45.930
and I joked that it was like that

2127
01:29:45.930 --> 01:29:47.790
the hand of God came over me.

2128
01:29:47.790 --> 01:29:50.640
And I literally felt like my head

2129
01:29:50.640 --> 01:29:52.350
started to float on my body

2130
01:29:52.350 --> 01:29:55.470
and I'm just like a machine those last 12 miles.

2131
01:29:55.470 --> 01:29:59.160
And it was kind of one of those points where I was

2132
01:29:59.160 --> 01:30:01.560
either gonna get the record or not get the record.

2133
01:30:01.560 --> 01:30:03.570
And I felt like I just found some sort of,

2134
01:30:03.570 --> 01:30:06.540
like, spiritual connection where my body just became

2135
01:30:06.540 --> 01:30:09.720
a machine and it was kind of like my head was floating.

2136
01:30:09.720 --> 01:30:12.030
And I felt that like many more times

2137
01:30:12.030 --> 01:30:13.350
when I've gone for world records.

2138
01:30:13.350 --> 01:30:15.720
I feel like, even I think about when I did

2139
01:30:15.720 --> 01:30:17.910
my 12 hour world record, I started to feel that.

2140
01:30:17.910 --> 01:30:19.620
I feel like I kind of just went

2141
01:30:19.620 --> 01:30:21.720
into this, like, machine mode.

2142
01:30:21.720 --> 01:30:25.860
And, yeah, I mean, it was like, it's incredible.

2143
01:30:25.860 --> 01:30:28.530
I just feel it, I feel this energy that I can

2144
01:30:28.530 --> 01:30:31.470
tap into when I go for these amazing things.

2145
01:30:31.470 --> 01:30:34.080
Like this advanced flow state where

2146
01:30:34.080 --> 01:30:36.664
you become this channel for something else.

2147
01:30:36.664 --> 01:30:38.232
Yeah, yeah.

2148
01:30:38.232 --> 01:30:40.950
I feel it, like, it's incredible.

2149
01:30:40.950 --> 01:30:43.440
Like, even racing Harvey, I mean, it was like,

2150
01:30:43.440 --> 01:30:46.170
I just transcended like this last couple miles

2151
01:30:46.170 --> 01:30:48.480
and I don't know how to describe it.

2152
01:30:48.480 --> 01:30:51.390
Like, I just, it's like I almost like detach a bit

2153
01:30:51.390 --> 01:30:54.120
from my body and from the pain I'm feeling

2154
01:30:54.120 --> 01:30:55.860
and I just become this machine.

2155
01:30:55.860 --> 01:30:57.780
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

2156
01:30:57.780 --> 01:30:58.613
That's wild.

2157
01:30:58.613 --> 01:31:00.730
Well, that's why you have to do the 3,100.

2158
01:31:00.730 --> 01:31:03.180
It is this self transcendent run.

2159
01:31:03.180 --> 01:31:04.013
Yes.

2160
01:31:04.013 --> 01:31:04.846
That's what it's called, right?

2161
01:31:04.846 --> 01:31:05.880
Yeah, absolutely.

2162
01:31:05.880 --> 01:31:10.233
I feel like that's gonna be like my finale as a runner.

2163
01:31:11.940 --> 01:31:15.120
How do you think about yourself as a role model?

2164
01:31:15.120 --> 01:31:17.790
Obviously, you're inspiring to everybody,

2165
01:31:17.790 --> 01:31:22.790
but particularly to women, women athletes, young girls.

2166
01:31:22.890 --> 01:31:24.390
I'm sure you have opportunities

2167
01:31:24.390 --> 01:31:26.100
to speak with younger people.

2168
01:31:26.100 --> 01:31:29.430
Like, how do you think about how you carry yourself

2169
01:31:29.430 --> 01:31:31.320
and what that means for the next

2170
01:31:31.320 --> 01:31:33.360
generation of young athletes?

2171
01:31:33.360 --> 01:31:36.180
Yeah, well, when I'm out there and I'm going,

2172
01:31:36.180 --> 01:31:37.830
I'm about to do something, I mean,

2173
01:31:37.830 --> 01:31:41.520
I think about that not only the people that inspire me

2174
01:31:41.520 --> 01:31:44.460
to do a race, I feel like every race that I do,

2175
01:31:44.460 --> 01:31:47.550
I have somebody that is inspiring me and that I think about.

2176
01:31:47.550 --> 01:31:49.380
Like, especially later in the race.

2177
01:31:49.380 --> 01:31:51.630
But I also know that people are watching me

2178
01:31:51.630 --> 01:31:54.360
and that I'm bringing an inspiration to other people.

2179
01:31:54.360 --> 01:31:57.390
And I feel that, I feel that during the race.

2180
01:31:57.390 --> 01:32:00.057
I feel like, you know, I'm doing this for the world.

2181
01:32:00.057 --> 01:32:02.670
I'm doing this to, you know, elevate the sport.

2182
01:32:02.670 --> 01:32:06.240
To raise the bar in what women think is possible.

2183
01:32:06.240 --> 01:32:09.990
And, like, it's such a powerful thing when you're out there.

2184
01:32:09.990 --> 01:32:11.820
I feel like it's not just me

2185
01:32:11.820 --> 01:32:13.530
trying to reach a personal goal.

2186
01:32:13.530 --> 01:32:15.180
I'm doing this for the sport.

2187
01:32:15.180 --> 01:32:16.800
I'm creating history.

2188
01:32:16.800 --> 01:32:20.340
And it just like, totally like elevates me,

2189
01:32:20.340 --> 01:32:22.920
like, later in the race, just thinking about that.

2190
01:32:22.920 --> 01:32:25.441
And now being 40, you're not just breaking barriers

2191
01:32:25.441 --> 01:32:29.310
in distance and time, you're breaking barriers in age.

2192
01:32:29.310 --> 01:32:32.100
Like, you haven't even begun to, you know,

2193
01:32:32.100 --> 01:32:34.020
tap into what you're capable of.

2194
01:32:34.020 --> 01:32:36.150
I'm sure you've got a whole slew of world records,

2195
01:32:36.150 --> 01:32:39.630
you know, coming, but it's really cool

2196
01:32:39.630 --> 01:32:42.870
because it creates this conversation around what

2197
01:32:42.870 --> 01:32:46.050
we're capable of and what our limits are as we age.

2198
01:32:46.050 --> 01:32:49.890
And, as you redefine it, you're in equal parts,

2199
01:32:49.890 --> 01:32:51.400
inspiring young people, as you are to,

2200
01:32:51.400 --> 01:32:54.704
like, the older people who are trying to, you know,

2201
01:32:54.704 --> 01:32:57.750
rethink what they're capable of as we get older.

2202
01:32:57.750 --> 01:32:58.583
Yeah, absolutely.

2203
01:32:58.583 --> 01:32:59.520
That's awesome.

2204
01:32:59.520 --> 01:33:02.490
I mean, I think about when I reached my mid thirties,

2205
01:33:02.490 --> 01:33:04.170
I thought it was on some sort of like

2206
01:33:04.170 --> 01:33:06.090
downhill slope or something.

2207
01:33:06.090 --> 01:33:08.670
And, I mean, probably a lot of like marathoners

2208
01:33:08.670 --> 01:33:11.130
and, you know, ultra athletes think that.

2209
01:33:11.130 --> 01:33:14.400
They think that, oh, you know, it's all downhill from here.

2210
01:33:14.400 --> 01:33:16.893
But I feel like I'm reaching my prime right now.

2211
01:33:16.893 --> 01:33:18.900
And, I mean, it's incredible because here

2212
01:33:18.900 --> 01:33:21.810
I've been a runner for 27 years

2213
01:33:21.810 --> 01:33:25.110
and I'm still getting better, you know, at age 40.

2214
01:33:25.110 --> 01:33:28.770
And, you know, I feel like I'm kind of dialing in more,

2215
01:33:28.770 --> 01:33:31.620
like I talked about my diet and my recovery.

2216
01:33:31.620 --> 01:33:34.920
I'm kind of dialing in more on those factors that,

2217
01:33:34.920 --> 01:33:37.770
you know, maybe I just kind of let them slide in the past.

2218
01:33:37.770 --> 01:33:38.603
Sure.

2219
01:33:38.603 --> 01:33:39.467
And wasn't really as dialed in on them.

2220
01:33:39.467 --> 01:33:41.340
So I'm kind of showing like, you know,

2221
01:33:41.340 --> 01:33:44.934
if I'm using like more of my brain and having more wisdom

2222
01:33:44.934 --> 01:33:48.090
with my training, my recovery, my diet,

2223
01:33:48.090 --> 01:33:50.470
all these things and it's, like, working.

2224
01:33:50.470 --> 01:33:52.050
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

2225
01:33:52.050 --> 01:33:54.780
And it's also cool that we're in a time where,

2226
01:33:54.780 --> 01:33:58.380
you know, somebody who is of your age

2227
01:33:58.380 --> 01:34:00.090
can live as a professional athlete.

2228
01:34:00.090 --> 01:34:02.730
Like, you have sponsors and you can make a living

2229
01:34:02.730 --> 01:34:07.470
and, you know, do your thing and I think that's amazing.

2230
01:34:08.320 --> 01:34:10.080
Like, that wouldn't have been possible,

2231
01:34:10.080 --> 01:34:12.330
you know, maybe even like 10 years ago, you know?

2232
01:34:12.330 --> 01:34:13.397
Oh yeah.

2233
01:34:13.397 --> 01:34:14.230
Yeah, oh definitely.

2234
01:34:14.230 --> 01:34:16.887
I mean, I like, I mean, I've been this sport for seven years

2235
01:34:16.887 --> 01:34:20.297
and I've seen this whole evolution with sponsorships.

2236
01:34:20.297 --> 01:34:24.750
Even when I first got into the sport, it was actually

2237
01:34:24.750 --> 01:34:27.842
hard to get sponsorships, even being a woman.

2238
01:34:27.842 --> 01:34:32.130
I felt like I saw, like, more younger men

2239
01:34:32.130 --> 01:34:34.320
getting sponsorship opportunities.

2240
01:34:34.320 --> 01:34:37.470
And I, like, here I was, I had done a lot

2241
01:34:37.470 --> 01:34:39.840
in, like, just my first year as an ultra runner

2242
01:34:39.840 --> 01:34:41.910
and I felt like that there was

2243
01:34:41.910 --> 01:34:43.770
kind of more this focus on men.

2244
01:34:43.770 --> 01:34:45.750
And it's kind of shifted now that

2245
01:34:45.750 --> 01:34:48.210
there's so many amazing women doing things

2246
01:34:48.210 --> 01:34:50.671
and sponsors have realized like, oh,

2247
01:34:50.671 --> 01:34:53.533
you know, like, we've got these amazing women

2248
01:34:53.533 --> 01:34:55.140
that, you know, we can back.

2249
01:34:55.140 --> 01:34:56.340
And I feel like there's kind of been

2250
01:34:56.340 --> 01:34:58.950
this shift of supporting more women.

2251
01:34:58.950 --> 01:35:01.479
And more women, more athletes of,

2252
01:35:01.479 --> 01:35:03.330
you know, all different ages as well.

2253
01:35:03.330 --> 01:35:04.823
I mean, I'm, you know, just turned 40

2254
01:35:04.823 --> 01:35:06.690
and I'm professional athlete.

2255
01:35:06.690 --> 01:35:08.580
And, I mean, I had a profession

2256
01:35:08.580 --> 01:35:10.379
before I was a professional athlete.

2257
01:35:10.379 --> 01:35:11.212
Right.

2258
01:35:11.212 --> 01:35:12.690
That, you know, I thought I would

2259
01:35:12.690 --> 01:35:14.910
be working in research for the rest of my life.

2260
01:35:14.910 --> 01:35:16.740
I couldn't have imagined that

2261
01:35:16.740 --> 01:35:19.237
I'm a professional athlete at age 40.

2262
01:35:19.237 --> 01:35:21.187
Like, this is just wild.

2263
01:35:21.187 --> 01:35:23.910
Right, like, were you working in a lab in like Ann Arbor,

2264
01:35:23.910 --> 01:35:25.860
like studying bone density or something?

2265
01:35:25.860 --> 01:35:26.693
Yeah.

2266
01:35:26.693 --> 01:35:28.920
Yeah, I worked at the University of Oklahoma

2267
01:35:28.920 --> 01:35:31.440
Health Science Center, but I also worked

2268
01:35:31.440 --> 01:35:34.470
at Michigan for a period back in 2016.

2269
01:35:34.470 --> 01:35:37.500
And so, I mean, I had a dedicated career,

2270
01:35:37.500 --> 01:35:40.770
like, I was not expecting to become this ultra runner,

2271
01:35:40.770 --> 01:35:43.380
this professional athlete, you know, at this age.

2272
01:35:43.380 --> 01:35:46.286
And I had to go through a transition

2273
01:35:46.286 --> 01:35:49.200
trying to transition from my profession,

2274
01:35:49.200 --> 01:35:52.267
my day job, to become a professional athlete.

2275
01:35:52.267 --> 01:35:54.450
I mean, it was like, oh my gosh, what do I do with this?

2276
01:35:54.450 --> 01:35:55.500
Right.

2277
01:35:55.500 --> 01:35:59.260
But, yeah, once I had my car accident back in 2019

2278
01:35:59.260 --> 01:36:02.940
and, I mean, I was driving to work that day

2279
01:36:02.940 --> 01:36:06.180
and I felt like it was almost like this sign from above

2280
01:36:06.180 --> 01:36:08.280
telling me, you know, this is where it stops.

2281
01:36:08.280 --> 01:36:11.737
Like, you know, go full time with your running.

2282
01:36:11.737 --> 01:36:13.492
And I mean, I'm grateful.

2283
01:36:13.492 --> 01:36:17.280
I feel like I'm doing what I'm born to do as a runner.

2284
01:36:17.280 --> 01:36:20.640
And it's empowering, like, you know, to be doing this.

2285
01:36:20.640 --> 01:36:22.080
And I'm grateful for that.

2286
01:36:22.080 --> 01:36:23.730
I wanna be the best that I can be

2287
01:36:23.730 --> 01:36:26.287
and to do everything right.

2288
01:36:26.287 --> 01:36:28.200
Yeah, and on a practical level,

2289
01:36:28.200 --> 01:36:31.260
so you have your sponsors and then,

2290
01:36:31.260 --> 01:36:33.480
do you guys also coach other people

2291
01:36:33.480 --> 01:36:34.890
or like, how does it work so that

2292
01:36:34.890 --> 01:36:36.377
all the bills get paid and you can travel?

2293
01:36:36.377 --> 01:36:38.850
'Cause it's expensive to travel around and go

2294
01:36:38.850 --> 01:36:40.680
to all these races and do all that kind of stuff.

2295
01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:41.513
Yeah, yeah.

2296
01:36:41.513 --> 01:36:44.010
Well, fortunately, I've got a lot of support from Hoka.

2297
01:36:44.010 --> 01:36:46.963
So, Hoka is my main sponsor and, you know,

2298
01:36:46.963 --> 01:36:49.350
I've got a travel budget with them.

2299
01:36:49.350 --> 01:36:52.112
But, yeah, we also coach on the side,

2300
01:36:52.112 --> 01:36:54.138
you know, which keeps my husband busy.

2301
01:36:54.138 --> 01:36:55.170
Yeah.

2302
01:36:55.170 --> 01:36:57.840
But, yeah, I mean, we coach together and, you know,

2303
01:36:57.840 --> 01:37:00.120
that's what I'm doing a lot in my free time, you know,

2304
01:37:00.120 --> 01:37:01.860
we're trying to work with our athletes.

2305
01:37:01.860 --> 01:37:05.040
And, yeah, just communication with athletes

2306
01:37:05.040 --> 01:37:06.690
and that sort of thing.

2307
01:37:06.690 --> 01:37:10.420
So, yeah, we started our coaching business, you know,

2308
01:37:10.420 --> 01:37:13.320
back when I was still working full time in research.

2309
01:37:13.320 --> 01:37:16.710
And, I mean, that was really helpful, you know,

2310
01:37:16.710 --> 01:37:18.540
trying to transition into becoming

2311
01:37:18.540 --> 01:37:20.850
a full-time athlete along with, you know,

2312
01:37:20.850 --> 01:37:23.874
having our own business was really beneficial.

2313
01:37:23.874 --> 01:37:26.700
But I feel it's so cool for me to be able

2314
01:37:26.700 --> 01:37:28.890
to give back my knowledge and my wisdom

2315
01:37:28.890 --> 01:37:31.110
to the running community and to see

2316
01:37:31.110 --> 01:37:33.685
other athletes, like, succeed.

2317
01:37:33.685 --> 01:37:37.260
We just coached the guy that, and Joe McConaughy

2318
01:37:37.260 --> 01:37:39.540
that just won the Cocodona 250.

2319
01:37:39.540 --> 01:37:43.020
I mean, he just crushed it here last week.

2320
01:37:43.020 --> 01:37:45.600
And, I mean, it's really cool to be able to use,

2321
01:37:45.600 --> 01:37:48.540
you know, my experience and my wisdom

2322
01:37:48.540 --> 01:37:50.220
to give back to other athletes and see

2323
01:37:50.220 --> 01:37:52.380
somebody like him, you know, just crush it.

2324
01:37:52.380 --> 01:37:53.520
Right, right, right.

2325
01:37:53.520 --> 01:37:56.190
On that note, what do you see, like,

2326
01:37:56.190 --> 01:37:58.680
a lot of people doing wrong or what are common mistakes

2327
01:37:58.680 --> 01:38:01.170
that the amateur athlete makes when

2328
01:38:01.170 --> 01:38:03.780
they're participating in these longer races?

2329
01:38:03.780 --> 01:38:07.350
Yeah, I mean, I would say as far as mistakes

2330
01:38:07.350 --> 01:38:09.390
that athletes make, I would say too many

2331
01:38:09.390 --> 01:38:12.360
athletes run their easy days too fast.

2332
01:38:12.360 --> 01:38:15.240
And so, we really have to pull back the reins

2333
01:38:15.240 --> 01:38:17.017
on the athletes that we coach

2334
01:38:17.017 --> 01:38:19.350
that people don't know how to run easy.

2335
01:38:19.350 --> 01:38:20.670
They don't know how to run relaxed.

2336
01:38:20.670 --> 01:38:22.181
Well, Strava doesn't help with that.

2337
01:38:22.181 --> 01:38:23.014
(Camille laughing)

2338
01:38:23.014 --> 01:38:23.847
That's true.

2339
01:38:23.847 --> 01:38:24.680
You know, 'cause every time you're out there,

2340
01:38:24.680 --> 01:38:26.070
you're like, what's this gonna look like on Strava?

2341
01:38:26.070 --> 01:38:27.960
I don't want to be judged for running slow.

2342
01:38:27.960 --> 01:38:29.913
Like, I don't think it's good for that, you know?

2343
01:38:29.913 --> 01:38:33.863
Yeah, I think it turns people into workout champs.

2344
01:38:33.863 --> 01:38:36.480
I mean, I look at Strava sometimes

2345
01:38:36.480 --> 01:38:39.210
and I'm like, oh my gosh, like it's mind boggling

2346
01:38:39.210 --> 01:38:41.303
some of the workouts that people are doing

2347
01:38:41.303 --> 01:38:43.260
and their easy days are hard.

2348
01:38:43.260 --> 01:38:44.730
Like, every day is, like, hard.

2349
01:38:44.730 --> 01:38:46.740
They don't have any concept of pulling

2350
01:38:46.740 --> 01:38:48.300
back the reins and running easy

2351
01:38:48.300 --> 01:38:50.160
on their easy days, hard on their hard days.

2352
01:38:50.160 --> 01:38:52.231
It seems like every day is hard.

2353
01:38:52.231 --> 01:38:54.720
So, yeah, I mean, with the athletes that we coach,

2354
01:38:54.720 --> 01:38:56.040
you know, we're really trying to get them

2355
01:38:56.040 --> 01:38:58.680
to pull back their pace on the easy runs.

2356
01:38:58.680 --> 01:39:00.780
But then, the other thing is

2357
01:39:00.780 --> 01:39:03.600
we don't do back to back long runs.

2358
01:39:03.600 --> 01:39:06.570
We don't believe in, we think it's too much stress.

2359
01:39:06.570 --> 01:39:09.870
And so, when we start coaching athletes and they're like,

2360
01:39:09.870 --> 01:39:11.820
you know, where's our 40 mile training run.

2361
01:39:11.820 --> 01:39:14.220
Right, that's a standard thing in ultra prep.

2362
01:39:14.220 --> 01:39:15.053
Yeah.

2363
01:39:15.053 --> 01:39:16.517
Like, doing a long run on tired legs.

2364
01:39:16.517 --> 01:39:17.449
Yeah.

2365
01:39:17.449 --> 01:39:19.230
So the back to back is a big part of that.

2366
01:39:19.230 --> 01:39:24.030
Yeah, so even coaching Joe for Cocodona, you know,

2367
01:39:24.030 --> 01:39:26.580
he thought he needed be to be doing a lot of burn,

2368
01:39:26.580 --> 01:39:28.860
he needed be doing a lot of long runs,

2369
01:39:28.860 --> 01:39:31.892
and we just really pulled back the reins with his training.

2370
01:39:31.892 --> 01:39:34.830
He's the type of guy that, he ran in college

2371
01:39:34.830 --> 01:39:37.080
so he has a lot of like natural leg speed.

2372
01:39:37.080 --> 01:39:39.783
And so, we worked more with his natural physiology

2373
01:39:39.783 --> 01:39:43.647
and he thrived on that, like, and he went into Cocodona

2374
01:39:43.647 --> 01:39:45.930
and he felt like he was pretty strong,

2375
01:39:45.930 --> 01:39:48.570
like, most of the race and I think a lot of it

2376
01:39:48.570 --> 01:39:50.670
is just that he's not over trained.

2377
01:39:50.670 --> 01:39:53.326
He went into the race, like, pretty fresh.

2378
01:39:53.326 --> 01:39:57.150
He had done a 100K, like, a couple weeks

2379
01:39:57.150 --> 01:40:00.561
before as a race as, like, his last, like, long run.

2380
01:40:00.561 --> 01:40:02.160
But then, you know, we had him

2381
01:40:02.160 --> 01:40:03.930
come back and do a speed session

2382
01:40:03.930 --> 01:40:07.060
a couple days after that and he just crushed it.

2383
01:40:07.060 --> 01:40:10.920
So, we kind of worked more with his natural physiology

2384
01:40:10.920 --> 01:40:12.420
rather than, you know, doing all these,

2385
01:40:12.420 --> 01:40:15.540
like, back to back long runs and extreme amounts of burn.

2386
01:40:15.540 --> 01:40:17.130
Right, but I also find myself thinking,

2387
01:40:17.130 --> 01:40:19.410
as somebody who doesn't have a background in running,

2388
01:40:19.410 --> 01:40:21.390
I have a background in swimming and other things,

2389
01:40:21.390 --> 01:40:24.360
but you having this, you know,

2390
01:40:24.360 --> 01:40:27.364
elite background and Joe is his name?

2391
01:40:27.364 --> 01:40:28.197
Yeah, Joe.

2392
01:40:28.197 --> 01:40:31.022
Having a background as a really good runner

2393
01:40:31.022 --> 01:40:33.480
makes it make more sense, like, okay,

2394
01:40:33.480 --> 01:40:35.970
you don't need to do your long runs longer than 22.

2395
01:40:35.970 --> 01:40:38.542
Like, 'cause you're tapping into, you know,

2396
01:40:38.542 --> 01:40:42.060
two decades or a decade plus of that type of background.

2397
01:40:42.060 --> 01:40:43.980
But if you're an amateur runner who's kind of new

2398
01:40:43.980 --> 01:40:46.049
to all of this and doesn't have that.

2399
01:40:46.049 --> 01:40:46.882
Yeah.

2400
01:40:46.882 --> 01:40:49.050
I think on a confidence level, they need to do a longer

2401
01:40:49.050 --> 01:40:51.767
run just to convince themselves, like, it's possible.

2402
01:40:51.767 --> 01:40:54.780
But maybe there's something, too, like those

2403
01:40:54.780 --> 01:40:58.770
back to back long hard runs that, you know,

2404
01:40:58.770 --> 01:41:02.130
that fills the gap because they didn't have that,

2405
01:41:02.130 --> 01:41:04.110
you know, experience as a younger person.

2406
01:41:04.110 --> 01:41:07.410
Yeah, I kind of feel like doing races, you know,

2407
01:41:07.410 --> 01:41:10.980
gives people the confidence and the way to prepare

2408
01:41:10.980 --> 01:41:13.686
for whatever sort of like A goal race.

2409
01:41:13.686 --> 01:41:15.120
Even I do that.

2410
01:41:15.120 --> 01:41:18.030
I mean, I like to, when I'm going for, you know,

2411
01:41:18.030 --> 01:41:20.460
training for a big race, like a world championship,

2412
01:41:20.460 --> 01:41:23.100
I like to do, like, a marathon or a 50K

2413
01:41:23.100 --> 01:41:26.190
a couple weeks out, you know, it's like my last,

2414
01:41:26.190 --> 01:41:28.320
like, long run, you know, for that big race.

2415
01:41:28.320 --> 01:41:30.240
And I mean, that's kind of the mindset

2416
01:41:30.240 --> 01:41:31.860
that we have coaching the athletes.

2417
01:41:31.860 --> 01:41:33.930
That we, you know, try to do, like, you know,

2418
01:41:33.930 --> 01:41:36.000
something big like a couple weeks

2419
01:41:36.000 --> 01:41:38.910
before their, like, A race and you know,

2420
01:41:38.910 --> 01:41:40.920
that kind of like helps them to practice,

2421
01:41:40.920 --> 01:41:43.320
you know, their nutrition and spending,

2422
01:41:43.320 --> 01:41:45.360
you know, more time on their feet.

2423
01:41:45.360 --> 01:41:46.950
But, yeah, I feel like doing all these,

2424
01:41:46.950 --> 01:41:48.300
like, back to back long runs,

2425
01:41:48.300 --> 01:41:50.190
I feel like it's too much stress.

2426
01:41:50.190 --> 01:41:52.410
And, I mean, I have a master's degree

2427
01:41:52.410 --> 01:41:55.183
in exercise and sports science and, I mean,

2428
01:41:55.183 --> 01:41:58.080
I just can't really justify from a science standpoint,

2429
01:41:58.080 --> 01:41:59.850
like, there's just too much stress,

2430
01:41:59.850 --> 01:42:01.890
like, from doing so many long runs.

2431
01:42:01.890 --> 01:42:03.570
I feel like it beats you down.

2432
01:42:03.570 --> 01:42:05.400
And when I've tried that, when I've tried

2433
01:42:05.400 --> 01:42:08.550
to do, like, 30 mile plus training runs,

2434
01:42:08.550 --> 01:42:10.740
I mean, I feel like I get tired.

2435
01:42:10.740 --> 01:42:14.190
I'm like, I don't feel like this is necessary.

2436
01:42:14.190 --> 01:42:16.830
And so, yeah, when I like pulled back the reins,

2437
01:42:16.830 --> 01:42:18.548
like, I feel like, you know,

2438
01:42:18.548 --> 01:42:19.381
if you pull back the reins and you know,

2439
01:42:19.381 --> 01:42:22.950
you're finding that perfect formula to have, like,

2440
01:42:22.950 --> 01:42:24.900
a race, like, a couple weeks out,

2441
01:42:24.900 --> 01:42:26.760
that that kind of gets you dialed in.

2442
01:42:26.760 --> 01:42:28.590
And then just, you know, taper and recover

2443
01:42:28.590 --> 01:42:29.500
for the race, the big race.

2444
01:42:29.500 --> 01:42:32.043
Yeah, yeah, that's really interesting.

2445
01:42:33.390 --> 01:42:35.880
The final thing I wanted to ask you about has

2446
01:42:35.880 --> 01:42:39.420
to do with diversity in ultra running.

2447
01:42:39.420 --> 01:42:41.040
I mean, obviously, it's a very kind

2448
01:42:41.040 --> 01:42:43.313
of, like, white sport, you know.

2449
01:42:43.313 --> 01:42:46.590
I think it is really cool that there's so much enthusiasm

2450
01:42:46.590 --> 01:42:49.200
and excitement around women in ultra running.

2451
01:42:49.200 --> 01:42:52.200
And that's where it's most exciting, I think,

2452
01:42:52.200 --> 01:42:53.790
because of people like you who are

2453
01:42:53.790 --> 01:42:56.070
really pushing boundaries and advancing

2454
01:42:56.070 --> 01:42:58.646
the conversation of what's possible.

2455
01:42:58.646 --> 01:43:01.860
But how can we make it a little bit more inclusive?

2456
01:43:01.860 --> 01:43:03.540
I know this is something that you care about,

2457
01:43:03.540 --> 01:43:06.240
you know, so elaborate on that a little bit.

2458
01:43:06.240 --> 01:43:07.777
Yeah, well, it's really interesting

2459
01:43:07.777 --> 01:43:11.130
when I go to Comrades and that Comrades

2460
01:43:11.130 --> 01:43:13.860
is probably the most diverse race in the world

2461
01:43:13.860 --> 01:43:16.620
as far as ultra running and yeah, and then

2462
01:43:16.620 --> 01:43:18.540
you look at trail running and things

2463
01:43:18.540 --> 01:43:21.870
and, you know, tend to be a little less diverse.

2464
01:43:21.870 --> 01:43:24.660
And I think one thing that Comrades has going

2465
01:43:24.660 --> 01:43:28.440
for it is they have a very small entry fee.

2466
01:43:28.440 --> 01:43:30.229
I don't know what the conversion is

2467
01:43:30.229 --> 01:43:33.810
for their amount to enter the race,

2468
01:43:33.810 --> 01:43:35.190
but they have a different entry fee

2469
01:43:35.190 --> 01:43:39.420
for international versus within South Africa.

2470
01:43:39.420 --> 01:43:42.120
And so, I think of, you know, money,

2471
01:43:42.120 --> 01:43:44.070
the cost of getting into a race.

2472
01:43:44.070 --> 01:43:46.320
Like, that's one barrier, for sure.

2473
01:43:46.320 --> 01:43:48.480
And I feel like, you know, maybe trial running

2474
01:43:48.480 --> 01:43:50.490
needs to have some sort of subsidized,

2475
01:43:50.490 --> 01:43:53.040
like, entry fees or something to, you know,

2476
01:43:53.040 --> 01:43:57.300
to allow like more people to afford it.

2477
01:43:57.300 --> 01:43:59.910
But, yeah, I think that, yeah, and I mean,

2478
01:43:59.910 --> 01:44:03.715
Comrades is kind of like brings in like a global, like,

2479
01:44:03.715 --> 01:44:07.530
I don't know, it just seems like they have like this, like,

2480
01:44:07.530 --> 01:44:08.940
like it's kind of like the world

2481
01:44:08.940 --> 01:44:10.740
coming together type of thing.

2482
01:44:10.740 --> 01:44:11.610
Yeah.

2483
01:44:11.610 --> 01:44:14.172
But, also, there's such a steep tradition.

2484
01:44:14.172 --> 01:44:15.480
Yeah.

2485
01:44:15.480 --> 01:44:16.590
There around it.

2486
01:44:16.590 --> 01:44:18.870
Like, everybody there knows about it and everybody,

2487
01:44:18.870 --> 01:44:21.510
like, it was the whole community just kind of does it

2488
01:44:21.510 --> 01:44:23.220
whether they're trained to do it or not.

2489
01:44:23.220 --> 01:44:26.910
Yeah, yeah, and I feel like, I don't know,

2490
01:44:26.910 --> 01:44:29.250
maybe, you know, there needs to be more collaboration

2491
01:44:29.250 --> 01:44:32.190
between like what Comrades is doing right

2492
01:44:32.190 --> 01:44:35.550
and trying to bring that more to trail running.

2493
01:44:35.550 --> 01:44:36.990
But, yeah, just try to, you know,

2494
01:44:36.990 --> 01:44:39.990
think about what are the barriers to allowing,

2495
01:44:39.990 --> 01:44:42.654
you know, more diverse, you know, community.

2496
01:44:42.654 --> 01:44:45.293
You know, I follow, like, the Black Trail Runners,

2497
01:44:45.293 --> 01:44:48.660
I think they're based in the UK and, you know,

2498
01:44:48.660 --> 01:44:50.280
just trying to create a community

2499
01:44:50.280 --> 01:44:53.141
to get more diversity into the sport.

2500
01:44:53.141 --> 01:44:56.373
There's also like the Native American Trail Runners.

2501
01:44:56.373 --> 01:44:58.799
I think I actually donated towards their cause.

2502
01:44:58.799 --> 01:45:01.833
You donated your prize money towards that, right?

2503
01:45:01.833 --> 01:45:04.410
Yeah, yeah, and I feel like this,

2504
01:45:04.410 --> 01:45:06.090
like I need to give back more.

2505
01:45:06.090 --> 01:45:07.110
Like, I feel like, you know,

2506
01:45:07.110 --> 01:45:09.480
even if I'm getting prize money or, you know,

2507
01:45:09.480 --> 01:45:11.760
what are the ways that I can give back to the sport?

2508
01:45:11.760 --> 01:45:14.250
You know, do I donate part of my prize money?

